- **Testability:** the `strategy` field is package-private, allowing plain JUnit tests to set it directly without Quarkus or Mockito.

### When to use

Use this pattern when: a library or platform module ships several bundled implementations of an interface; only one can be active at a time; the choice belongs to config (not classpath composition); and external consumers should be able to replace the whole thing with their own implementation.

Do **not** use this for SPIs that consumers are expected to extend — use `@DefaultBean` directly on the no-op class for those (the standard consumer-replaceable SPI pattern).

### Why non-obvious

The instinct is to use `@Alternative` / `selected-alternatives` or `@Priority` on the individual strategy classes. Those approaches either require explicit config per alternative, create CDI ambiguity between bundled implementations, or make all implementations always-on. The producer pattern cleanly separates "which one" (config) from "how CDI wires it" (producer), and the strategy classes stay as plain testable POJOs.

**See also:** [GE-20260515-fd3156](GE-20260515-fd3156.md) — @DefaultBean must be on the @Produces method, not on the producer class

**See also:** [GE-20260427-543663](../quarkus/GE-20260427-543663.md) — @Produces @DefaultBean for library-level overridable CDI defaults

**See also:** [GE-20260601-fcf0d9](GE-20260601-fcf0d9.md) — two @DefaultBean beans for same type → AmbiguousResolutionException

@DefaultBean on @Produces method makes the produced bean default — placing it on the class does not

### What you observe

Placing `@DefaultBean` on the producer class rather than the `@Produces` method makes the *producer bean itself* a default bean — but the *produced bean* (the type returned by `@Produces`) remains a regular non-default `@ApplicationScoped` bean. A consumer-provided `@ApplicationScoped` bean of the same type then conflicts, causing CDI ambiguity at boot.

### Context

Any Quarkus ARC application using a CDI producer (`@Produces`) to supply a bean that should yield automatically to consumer-provided overrides.

### What was tried

```java
// WRONG — @DefaultBean on the class makes the PRODUCER BEAN default,
// not the produced ContextDiffStrategy bean
@DefaultBean
@ApplicationScoped
class ContextDiffStrategyProducer {
    @Produces
    @ApplicationScoped
    ContextDiffStrategy produce() { ... }
}
```

With this, a consumer's `@ApplicationScoped ContextDiffStrategy` causes CDI ambiguity — the produced bean is not a default bean and does not yield.

### Root cause

`@DefaultBean` applies to the bean it directly annotates. On a producer *method*, it applies to the produced bean. On the producer *class*, it applies to the producer class bean itself — not to what the producer produces.

### Fix

Place `@DefaultBean` on the `@Produces` method, not the class:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
class ContextDiffStrategyProducer {

@Produces
    @DefaultBean         // ← on the method, not the class
    @ApplicationScoped
    ContextDiffStrategy produce() { ... }
}
```

The produced `ContextDiffStrategy` is now a default bean. Any consumer `@ApplicationScoped ContextDiffStrategy` wins automatically without configuration.

### Why this is non-obvious

The `@DefaultBean` annotation is Quarkus ARC-specific (`io.quarkus.arc.DefaultBean`) and its producer-method vs producer-class placement is not explained in the Quarkus guides. The difference only surfaces as a CDI ambiguity error when a consumer tries to override the produced bean — the error doesn't point to the placement mistake.

**See also:** [GE-20260515-99cf39](GE-20260515-99cf39.md) — config-driven @Produces @DefaultBean for strategy selection

**See also:** [GE-20260422-e48245](GE-20260422-e48245.md) — @DefaultBean lives in io.quarkus.arc, not jakarta.enterprise.inject

**See also:** [GE-20260601-fcf0d9](GE-20260601-fcf0d9.md) — two @DefaultBean beans for the same type → AmbiguousResolutionException

**See also:** [GE-20260609-8d6961](GE-20260609-8d6961.md) — @Alternative @Qualifier on bean class restricts CDI injection

**See also:** [GE-20260609-d1feba](GE-20260609-d1feba.md) — CDI qualifier annotations belong in the injection-point module, not the producer module

