Store configuration on the runtime entity to eliminate a parallel callback registry

## Store configuration on the runtime entity to eliminate a parallel callback registry

**ID:** GE-20260513-e04f26
**Stack:** Quarkus CDI, Java 21
**Labels:** `#design` `#event-driven` `#state-management`
**What it achieves:** Eliminates a CDI singleton registry bean by storing callback configuration directly on the existing domain entity, making it restart-safe and lifecycle-correct automatically.
**Context:** An event handler needs to look up configuration at callback/completion time — e.g. a `WorkItemLifecycleAdapter` needs the `outputMapping` expression when a WorkItem completes. The configuration was set at schedule time and must be retrieved at completion time.

### The technique

Store the configuration on the existing domain entity rather than in a parallel CDI registry.

**Before (parallel registry approach):**
```java
// CDI singleton — separate parallel state
@ApplicationScoped
class HumanTaskRegistry {
    private final Map<String, HumanTaskTarget> registry = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

public void register(UUID caseId, String planItemId, HumanTaskTarget target) {
        registry.put(key(caseId, planItemId), target);
    }

public HumanTaskTarget get(UUID caseId, String planItemId) {
        return registry.get(key(caseId, planItemId));
    }
}
```

**After (entity-stored approach):**
```java
// Configuration lives on the entity — accessible wherever the entity is
public class PlanItem {
    private final BindingTarget target;  // stored at creation time

public BindingTarget target() { return target; }
}

// At callback time — no registry needed
void onWorkItemLifecycle(WorkItemLifecycleEvent event) {
    CallerRef ref = CallerRef.parse(workItem.callerRef);
    CasePlanModel plan = registry.get(ref.caseId()).orElse(null);
    PlanItem planItem = plan.getPlanItem(ref.planItemId()).orElse(null);

if (planItem.target() instanceof HumanTaskTarget t && t.outputMapping() != null) {
        Map<String, Object> output = t.outputMapping().evaluate(workItem.resolution);
        instance.getCaseContext().putAll(output);
    }
}
```

### Alternatives considered
- CDI singleton `ConcurrentHashMap<K, Config>` — explicit and familiar, but introduces parallel state management, restart durability concerns (state lost on restart), and memory leaks if cleanup is missed
- Storing config in the WorkItem metadata/labels — durable, but leaks engine-internal configuration into the casehub-work domain model (wrong layer)

### Why this is non-obvious
When a callback handler needs configuration from registration time, the instinct is a CDI registry bean. This is the standard pattern for correlation in event-driven systems and works correctly. The insight is that if a stable domain entity already tracks the same lifecycle, the entity IS the registry — storing config on it eliminates an entire CDI bean, makes the state restart-safe automatically, and makes the configuration available for all entity instances without explicit lifecycle management.

The pattern also generalises: in this case, storing `BindingTarget` on `PlanItem` (instead of just `planItemId` and `workerName`) made `outputMapping` available for ALL binding types, not just `HumanTaskTarget` — a capability improvement that a separate registry could not have provided without additional per-type registries.

### When to use it
- A callback handler needs configuration that was set at registration/schedule time
- A stable domain entity tracks the same lifecycle as the configuration (both are created together and live as long)
- The entity is accessible at callback time via an existing lookup path
- The configuration is inherently per-entity-instance (not shared across instances)

Do not use when the entity may be evicted or GC'd before the callback fires, or when the configuration spans multiple unrelated entity types.

*Score: 10/15 · Included because: eliminates a class of parallel-state management bugs, generalises beyond the specific Quarkus case, non-obvious alternative to the standard registry pattern · Reservation: requires entity accessibility at callback time*

CamelCase compound preservation in BM25 tokenization — exact identifier matches outrank partial via IDF

### Symptom

BM25 keyword search over code-domain text fails to distinguish exact identifier matches from coincidental word matches. Searching for `ChatModel` ranks documents containing unrelated "chat" and "model" equally to documents about the `ChatModel` Java interface.

### Context

Standard BM25 tokenizers (word-boundary split + lowercase) produce `chat` and `model` from `ChatModel`. A document about "a chat room model" matches identically to one about `dev.langchain4j.model.chat.ChatModel`. The compound identifier is destroyed.

### Fix

Produce both the compound form AND the components during tokenization:

```
ChatModel → [chatmodel, chat, model]
DefaultBean → [defaultbean, default, bean]
HTTPClient → [httpclient, http, client]
```

The compound token `chatmodel` is rare (high IDF) — it appears only in documents that actually discuss `ChatModel`. The component tokens `chat` and `model` are common (low IDF). BM25's IDF weighting naturally ranks documents matching the rare compound higher than documents matching only the common components.

```java
static List<String> tokenize(String text) {
    List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();
    String[] segments = text.split("[^a-zA-Z0-9]+");
    for (String segment : segments) {
        if (segment.isEmpty()) continue;
        List<String> parts = splitCamelCase(segment);
        for (String part : parts) tokens.add(part.toLowerCase());
        if (parts.size() > 1) tokens.add(segment.toLowerCase());
    }
    return tokens;
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The standard approach to CamelCase tokenization is to split into components only — `ChatModel → [chat, model]`. Keeping the compound alongside the components seems redundant until you realize IDF does the ranking work: the compound is always rarer than its components, so BM25 automatically favors exact matches without any special scoring logic. The insight is that the tokenizer controls ranking quality through IDF statistics, not through scoring formulas.

@CacheResult cache key omits tenancyId — cached data leaks across tenants

### Symptom

Tenant-A queries a report endpoint. Tenant-B queries the same endpoint moments later and receives tenant-A's data. No error, no warning — the cache silently returns the wrong tenant's results.

### Context

A multi-tenant Quarkus application uses `@CacheResult(cacheName = "reports")` on service methods that query tenant-scoped data. The cache key is derived from method parameters (e.g. report type, date range) but does not include the caller's tenancyId.

### What was tried (didn't work)

Initially assumed the problem was in the query layer — the store-level tenant filtering was correct. The cached result pre-empted the query entirely.

### Root cause

`@CacheResult` generates cache keys from method parameters only. When two tenants call the same method with the same parameters, the second call gets a cache hit from the first tenant's result. The cache has no awareness of the request-scoped `CurrentPrincipal.tenancyId()`.

### Fix

Remove `@CacheResult` from any method that returns tenant-scoped data. If caching is required, build a tenant-aware `CacheKeyGenerator` that includes `tenancyId` in the key:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class TenantAwareCacheKeyGenerator implements CacheKeyGenerator {
    @Inject CurrentPrincipal principal;

@Override
    public Object generate(Method method, Object... params) {
        return new CompositeCacheKey(principal.tenancyId(), params);
    }
}
```

Then annotate: `@CacheResult(cacheName = "reports", keyGenerator = TenantAwareCacheKeyGenerator.class)`.

The simpler fix — just remove `@CacheResult` — is appropriate when the cache hit rate doesn't justify the complexity.

### Why this is non-obvious

`@CacheResult` is a transparent annotation — developers add it for performance without thinking about what constitutes the cache key. In a single-tenant app this is fine. In a multi-tenant app, the default key generator creates a cross-tenant data leak with zero visible symptoms.

### Why this fix

Removing `@CacheResult` is preferred over the custom key generator because it eliminates the attack surface entirely. A tenant-aware key generator still requires every cached method to use it consistently — one missed annotation parameter and the leak returns.

### Alternatives considered

- **Cache per tenant** (separate cache instances): high memory overhead, complex eviction
- **Include tenancyId as explicit method parameter**: pollutes APIs, easy to forget
- **Remove caching entirely**: chosen as the pragmatic fix; re-add selectively with tenant-aware keys if profiling shows a need

ChatModel.doChat(ChatRequest) is the override point for test doubles — not chat(ChatRequest)

## The technique

When implementing a test `ChatModel` without mocks (because `AiMessage`/`ChatResponse` have final methods), override `doChat(ChatRequest)` — not `chat(ChatRequest)`:

```java
ChatModel testModel = new ChatModel() {
    @Override
    public ChatResponse doChat(final ChatRequest request) {
        // inspect request, return appropriate response
        return ChatResponse.builder()
                .aiMessage(new AiMessage("{...json...}"))
                .build();
    }
};
```

`chat(ChatRequest)` is a **default method** in the `ChatModel` interface that calls `chat(ChatRequest, ChatRequestOptions)`, which then calls `doChat(ChatRequest)`. The chain applies listeners, merges request parameters, and fires `onRequest`/`onResponse` hooks. By overriding `doChat()`, the test skips the listener chain and returns directly.

`casehub-engine-api`'s `Agent.execute()` calls `model.chat(request)` which eventually reaches `doChat()`.

## Why this is non-obvious

`chat(ChatRequest)` and `chat(ChatRequest, ChatRequestOptions)` are the visible API methods that developers see in IDE autocompletion. `doChat(ChatRequest)` is buried as an implementation detail — a `default` method with a `throw new RuntimeException("Not implemented")` body that exists specifically as the override target. The JavaDoc doesn't prominently surface this as the intended extension point for test doubles.

**See also:** [GE-20260618-5008f5](../casehub-engine/GE-20260618-5008f5.md) — @Alternative @Priority(10) CDI test bean avoids Quarkus CDI restart (related CDI testing technique)

InMemoryMemoryStore.assertTenant() fails silently in @ObservesAsync threads — no CurrentPrincipal in async CDI context

### Symptom

`CaseMemoryStore.store()` or `storeAll()` called from an `@ObservesAsync` CDI handler silently fails — no exception visible to the caller (if the caller catches and swallows), no facts stored. The `InMemoryMemoryStore` (and all adapters) calls `MemoryPermissions.assertTenant(input.tenantId(), principal)` which reads `CurrentPrincipal.tenancyId()`. In the async observer thread, there is no request scope — `CurrentPrincipal` is either unavailable or returns a default that doesn't match the input's tenantId.

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyEmitter {
    @Inject Instance<CaseMemoryStore> store;

void onEvent(@ObservesAsync SomeEvent event) {
        var s = store.get();
        try {
            s.storeAll(facts);  // silently fails — assertTenant throws SecurityException
        } catch (Exception e) {
            LOG.warn("swallowed");  // this fires, but the caller sees no problem
        } finally {
            store.destroy(s);
        }
    }
}
```

### Context

CDI `@ObservesAsync` handlers run on a managed executor thread, not the request thread. Quarkus does not propagate request-scoped beans (like `CurrentPrincipal`) to async observer threads by default. The `InMemoryMemoryStore` is `@ApplicationScoped` with `CurrentPrincipal` injected via constructor — but the principal's request-scoped state is empty/default in the async context.

All CaseMemoryStore adapters (InMemory, JPA, SQLite, Mem0) call `MemoryPermissions.assertTenant()` as a security guard. The tenantId is already in the `MemoryInput` — the assertion is a double-check against the calling principal's tenant, but in async context there is no calling principal.

### What was tried (didn't work)

1. **Using the NoOp store in async paths** — NoOp works (returns "" from store()) but defeats the purpose of testing memory emission.
2. **Setting FixedCurrentPrincipal.tenancyId in @BeforeEach** — only affects the test thread, not the async observer thread.

### Root cause

`MemoryPermissions.assertTenant()` reads `CurrentPrincipal.tenancyId()` which requires an active request scope. `@ObservesAsync` runs outside request scope on the managed executor. The tenantId is available in the `MemoryInput` but the adapter doesn't trust it — it asserts against the principal.

### Fix

Three options (none shipped yet — tracked as devtown#67):

1. **SmallRye Context Propagation** — configure `@ObservesAsync` to propagate `CurrentPrincipal` to async threads. Platform-level fix.
2. **Accept tenantId from MemoryInput** — adapters already have `input.tenantId()`. Skip assertTenant when no request scope is active. Platform-level fix.
3. **Test-scoped store** — `@Alternative` CaseMemoryStore in test sources that skips tenant assertion. Test-only workaround.

### Why this is non-obvious

The failure is completely silent. The emitter's catch-all logs a WARN, but since memory is enrichment (not critical path), the application continues normally. Facts are simply never stored. The integration test times out waiting for facts that will never arrive — the timeout is the only signal, and it could be mistaken for a slow async chain rather than a security assertion failure.

Canonical CloudEvent adapter pattern — 7 rules for CDI async adapters

Seven rules every CloudEvent adapter in the platform must satisfy.
Discovered during casehubio/connectors#20 — two existing adapters (IoT, Qhorus)
had 2 bugs, 1 silent failure path, and 1 pattern inconsistency. All fixed together.

### The pattern

| Rule | Requirement | Failure mode |
|------|-------------|-------------|
| ObjectMapper | Injected — never a private static instance | Static instance isolates serialisation config from app's shared mapper; custom serializers invisible |
| tenancyId | Null-safe — extension omitted when null | SDK serialises null extension as literal `"null"` or throws NPE |
| fireAsync | Always `.exceptionally(ex -> { LOG.warn(...); return null; })` | Unhandled `CompletionStage` swallows downstream observer failures silently |
| Serialisation error | Catch at WARN, fire with `new byte[0]` — never throw | Unchecked exception from `@ObservesAsync` propagates into CDI managed executor and is silently swallowed |
| type derivation | From a stable semantic field on the event record | Instance IDs are mutable; CloudEvent `type` is used for routing rules that must be stable |
| Severity | WARN for degraded paths | SEVERE causes alert fatigue; serialisation failure is degraded, not catastrophic |
| datacontenttype | Set `application/json` when data is ObjectMapper-serialised | Consumers cannot parse the `data` field without knowing its format; omitting it forces guessing |

`tenancyId` always sourced from the event record — never from `CurrentPrincipal`,
which is `@RequestScoped` and inactive in `@ObservesAsync` observers.

### Why this is non-obvious

Rules 3 and 4 interact: `fireAsync()` returns a `CompletionStage` that swallows
failures silently, but a thrown exception from the observer method *also* vanishes
into CDI's managed executor. Most developers add `.exceptionally()` (rule 3) but
don't realise that a throw *before* `fireAsync` is called (rule 4 — serialisation
error) is equally invisible. The error paths are different but the symptom is identical:
the event silently disappears with no log entry.

### Why this fix

The alternative — letting exceptions propagate — appears correct because CDI
"should" handle observer exceptions. But `@ObservesAsync` runs on a managed
executor thread where exceptions propagate into a `CompletionStage` that nobody
awaits. The pattern of catch-log-continue is the only way to make failures visible.

### Fix

Reference implementations:
- `QhorusCloudEventAdapter` (casehub-qhorus)
- `ConnectorsCloudEventAdapter` (casehub-connectors)
- `IoTCloudEventAdapter` (casehub-iot)

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class ExampleCloudEventAdapter {

private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(ExampleCloudEventAdapter.class);

private final Event<CloudEvent> cloudEventBus;
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;  // rule 1: injected

@Inject
    public ExampleCloudEventAdapter(Event<CloudEvent> cloudEventBus, ObjectMapper objectMapper) {
        this.cloudEventBus = cloudEventBus;
        this.objectMapper = objectMapper;
    }

public void onEvent(@ObservesAsync SomeDomainEvent event) {
        byte[] data;
        try {
            data = objectMapper.writeValueAsBytes(event);
        } catch (JsonProcessingException e) {
            LOG.warnf("Serialisation failed: %s", e.getMessage());  // rule 4 + 6
            data = new byte[0];
        }

CloudEventBuilder builder = CloudEventBuilder.v1()
                .withId(UUID.randomUUID().toString())
                .withType("io.casehub.domain." + event.semanticType())  // rule 5
                .withSource(URI.create("/source"))
                .withTime(event.occurredAt().atOffset(ZoneOffset.UTC))
                .withDataContentType("application/json")
                .withData(data);

if (event.tenancyId() != null) {  // rule 2
            builder = builder.withExtension("tenancyid", event.tenancyId());
        }

cloudEventBus.fireAsync(builder.build())
                .exceptionally(ex -> {  // rule 3 + 6
                    LOG.warnf(ex, "CloudEvent dispatch failed");
                    return null;
                });
    }
}
```

### ⚠️ Rule 3 exception — reactive messaging ack chains

Rule 3 (`.exceptionally()`) **must NOT be applied** when the `CompletionStage` is
chained to a message acknowledgement, as in SmallRye Reactive Messaging `@Incoming`
consumers:

```java
// WRONG — .exceptionally() swallows the exception; message.ack() always runs → silent data loss
return cloudEventBus.fireAsync(ce)
    .exceptionally(ex -> { LOG.warnf(ex, "dispatch failed"); return null; })
    .thenCompose(ignored -> message.ack());

// CORRECT — .whenComplete() preserves the exception; thenCompose skips on failure → SmallRye nacks
return cloudEventBus.fireAsync(ce)
    .whenComplete((v, t) -> { if (t != null) LOG.warnf(t, "dispatch failed"); })
    .thenCompose(ignored -> message.ack());
```

With `.exceptionally()`: exception swallowed → `null` returned → `thenCompose` fires →
`message.ack()` runs → message permanently consumed regardless of dispatch outcome.

With `.whenComplete()`: exception preserved on the stage → `thenCompose` skips its lambda
→ exception surfaces to SmallRye → message nacked → retried or dead-lettered.

Rule 3 applies **only in pure fire-and-forget dispatch** (the `CompletionStage` is not
returned or chained further). CDI `@ObservesAsync` adapters are always fire-and-forget.
SmallRye `@Incoming` consumers return the stage for ack/nack signalling — they are not.

### Alternatives considered

TenantContextRunner must save/restore TenantHolder when CDI request context already active

### Symptom

A background job processes items for tenant-A. Midway through, it calls a method that itself runs work in a tenant context (nested call). After the nested call returns, the outer job silently continues processing under the nested call's tenant identity — or worse, under no tenant at all.

### Context

`TenantContextRunner` programmatically activates a CDI request context, sets `TenantHolder.setTenancyId()`, runs a callback, then tears down the context. When the context is already active (e.g. called from within a REST request or another `TenantContextRunner` invocation), the runner must not terminate the outer context on exit.

### What was tried (didn't work)

Initial implementation checked `isActive()` before activating and skipped activation if already active. This handled the context lifecycle correctly but did not save/restore the tenant identity — the inner call's tenancyId persisted after return.

### Root cause

`Arc.container().requestContext().isActive()` correctly detects nesting, and `activate()`/`terminate()` are correctly skipped. But `TenantHolder.setTenancyId()` mutates the existing request-scoped bean — it doesn't create a new scope. Without explicit save/restore, the outer context's tenant identity is permanently overwritten.

### Fix

Save the previous tenancyId when the context is already active, restore it in `finally`:

```java
public <T> T runInTenantContext(String tenancyId, Supplier<T> work) {
    ManagedContext reqCtx = Arc.container().requestContext();
    boolean alreadyActive = reqCtx.isActive();

// Save previous state ONLY if context already active
    String previousTenancyId = null;
    if (alreadyActive) {
        previousTenancyId = tenantHolder.getTenancyId();
    } else {
        reqCtx.activate();
    }

try {
        tenantHolder.setTenancyId(tenancyId);
        return work.get();
    } finally {
        if (alreadyActive) {
            tenantHolder.setTenancyId(previousTenancyId); // restore
        } else {
            reqCtx.terminate();
        }
    }
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The CDI request context lifecycle (`activate`/`terminate`/`isActive`) is well-documented. The problem is that `@RequestScoped` beans are shared within an active context — they don't stack. Most developers assume that "checking isActive and skipping activation" is sufficient for safe nesting, but the scoped bean's mutable state is a separate concern that the context API doesn't protect.

@DefaultBean + plain @ApplicationScoped enables CDI layer displacement without config switches or @Alternative @Priority

## Context

You have a default implementation of an interface (a naive baseline, a no-op, a stub) that should be active unless a richer implementation is present. The richer implementation may be added in a future layer, by a downstream module, or conditionally.

## The technique

Mark the default implementation `@ApplicationScoped @DefaultBean`. Mark the richer implementation `@ApplicationScoped` only (no qualifier, no `@Alternative`, no `@Priority`).

```java
// Layer 1 — active only when no other bean of this type exists
@ApplicationScoped
@DefaultBean
public class NaiveAmlInvestigationService implements AmlInvestigationApplicationService { ... }

// Layer 2 — silently wins; NaiveAmlInvestigationService becomes dormant
@ApplicationScoped
public class WorkItemAmlInvestigationService implements AmlInvestigationApplicationService { ... }
```

CDI resolves to `WorkItemAmlInvestigationService` when both are present. `NaiveAmlInvestigationService` is still a valid CDI bean — injecting it by concrete type works fine — but it is no longer the resolution target for the interface.

## Why this is non-obvious

Developers reaching for "replaceable default" typically use `@Alternative @Priority(N)` (verbose, requires `@Priority` on the override) or profile-based activation (`@IfBuildProperty`, evaluated at build time only). `@DefaultBean` is simpler: the mere presence of any other bean of the same type suppresses it. No annotation on the override, no configuration, no profile.

## Constraints

- Both beans must be in the same Quarkus application (same augmentation pass).
- If you need to inject the `@DefaultBean` implementation directly by concrete type alongside the override, that works — CDI resolves by the concrete class, not by the interface.
- `@DefaultBean` is a Quarkus ARC extension, not CDI standard — import from `io.quarkus.arc.DefaultBean`.

