@ApplicationScoped CDI bean captures immutable-record snapshot at construction — registry updates are invisible

## @ApplicationScoped CDI bean captures immutable-record snapshot at construction — registry updates are invisible

**ID:** GE-20260604-b561df
**Stack:** Quarkus CDI, Java 21 (records)
**Labels:** `#cdi` `#applicationscoped` `#registry` `#snapshot` `#stale-state`

### Symptom

An `@ApplicationScoped` CDI bean holds a `final ImmutableRecord session` field. External callers update a backing registry (e.g. `ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfPresent(id, s -> s.withField(newValue))`) which replaces the map entry with a new record instance. The bean's `session` field still points to the original snapshot — silently. No exception, no warning. Any data derived from the snapshot (e.g. selection context) remains perpetually stale.

### Context

Pattern: a factory or lifecycle manager constructs a CDI bean and passes in an immutable record at construction time. The record is also stored in a registry keyed by an ID. Later, update operations target the registry entry (replacing the map value) rather than the bean's field.

Occurs whenever:
- The bean is `@ApplicationScoped` (constructed once)
- The constructor argument is an immutable record (or any value type)
- The registry stores the canonical, live value but the bean holds its own copy

### What was tried

The registry `updateSelection()` call was verified to work: `computeIfPresent` did replace the map entry with a new record carrying the updated selection. The bug was not in the registry — it was that the already-constructed CDI bean had captured the pre-update snapshot and never re-read from the registry.

### Root cause

CDI `@ApplicationScoped` beans are proxied and constructed once. A constructor parameter (or field set during construction) is a snapshot of the value at that moment. Java immutable records cannot be mutated in-place. When the registry replaces its entry, the bean holds a stale reference to the previous record. There is no observer mechanism to notify the bean that the registry entry was replaced.

### Fix

Inject the registry itself (not the record) and resolve the live value on each method invocation:

```java
// Before — captures a snapshot at construction; update_selection changes are invisible
public class ReviewerChannelBackend implements ChannelBackend {
    private final ReviewSession session;  // stale after any registry update

ReviewerChannelBackend(ReviewSession session, ...) {
        this.session = session;  // snapshot — never updated again
    }

public void post(...) {
        String context = buildSelectionContext(this.session);  // always stale
    }
}

// After — reads live session from registry on every invocation
public class ReviewerChannelBackend implements ChannelBackend {
    private final ReviewSessionRegistry registry;
    private final UUID channelId;

ReviewerChannelBackend(ReviewSessionRegistry registry, UUID channelId, ...) {
        this.registry = registry;
        this.channelId = channelId;
    }

public void post(...) {
        ReviewSession session = registry.find(channelId).orElse(null);
        if (session == null) return;  // session ended — silently ignore
        String context = buildSelectionContext(session);  // always live
    }
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The registry update (`computeIfPresent`) is correct and the map entry is genuinely replaced. The bug is not in the update path — it's in the consuming bean having captured the old value. This looks like the selection update "didn't work" rather than "worked but the result was never read." The indirection through a CDI proxy obscures when construction happened. Expert Quarkus developers know that `@ApplicationScoped` beans are singletons but don't instinctively reach for "is my injected value a stale snapshot?" when debugging.

### Why inject registry over record

Injecting the registry (and resolving on each call) ensures the consumer always sees the canonical live value. The overhead is a single map lookup per invocation — negligible for event-driven handlers. Passing the record directly at construction is convenient but creates invisible coupling between the construction-time state and the consuming bean's view of the world.

*Score: 11/15 · Included because: completely silent failure that looks like a feature isn't working; expert CDI developers would reach for injection patterns without instinctively suspecting snapshot capture; the fix is non-obvious (must restructure the constructor) · Reservation: the CDI singleton-per-context rule is well-known; the missing piece is applying it to snapshot semantics specifically*

Recursive deep copy of Map<String,Object> stops at List values — nested maps inside lists are aliased

### Symptom

After deep-copying a `Map<String, Object>`, mutating a `Map` that was inside a `List` value of the original modifies the copy, and vice versa. The snapshot is corrupted without any exception.

### Context

Any deep-copy utility that recursively processes a `Map<String, Object>` where values may be `Map`, `List`, or both nested arbitrarily. Common in serialization shims, context snapshots, and blackboard state copies.

### What was tried (didn't work)

```java
// Naive — only handles top-level Maps
private static Map<String, Object> deepCopy(Map<String, Object> source) {
    Map<String, Object> copy = new LinkedHashMap<>();
    for (var e : source.entrySet()) {
        Object v = e.getValue();
        if (v instanceof Map) v = deepCopy((Map<String, Object>) v);
        else if (v instanceof List) v = new ArrayList<>((List<?>) v); // WRONG — shallow
        copy.put(e.getKey(), v);
    }
    return copy;
}
```

A list like `workers: [{name: "extractor", runs: 2}]` is copied as `new ArrayList<>(workers)` — the list is new but the map entries inside it point to the same objects.

### Root cause

`new ArrayList<>(list)` copies list element references, not list elements. For lists of scalars (`String`, `Integer`) this is safe because Java scalars are immutable. For lists of maps, the map references are shared between the original and copy.

### Fix

Recursively deep-copy `Map` elements inside lists:

```java
@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
private static Map<String, Object> deepCopy(Map<String, Object> source) {
    Map<String, Object> copy = new LinkedHashMap<>();
    for (var e : source.entrySet()) {
        Object v = e.getValue();
        if (v instanceof Map) {
            v = deepCopy((Map<String, Object>) v);
        } else if (v instanceof List<?> list) {
            List<Object> listCopy = new ArrayList<>(list.size());
            for (Object item : list) {
                listCopy.add(item instanceof Map ? deepCopy((Map<String, Object>) item) : item);
            }
            v = listCopy;
        }
        copy.put(e.getKey(), v);
    }
    return copy;
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The recursion handles Maps correctly — every developer checks for nested maps. Lists feel like a terminal case because `new ArrayList<>(other)` is the standard copy idiom and works correctly for lists of scalars (immutable). The failure only manifests with lists-of-maps, which are a common structure in JSON-derived data (arrays of objects) but not always present in unit tests that only use simple scalar values.

Read-modify-write on thread-safe store is not atomic — concurrent writers lose updates

### Symptom

Under concurrent writes, a list value in a thread-safe store ends up with fewer entries than expected — some updates are silently lost. The store itself is thread-safe but the caller's update is not.

### Context

Any pattern where a caller (a) reads a mutable value from a thread-safe store, (b) modifies it outside the store, then (c) writes it back. Common with lists that are stored as values in a `ConcurrentHashMap` or similar.

### What was tried (didn't work)

```java
// RACE: two threads both read the same list, modify it, write back — last write wins
public static void recordWorkerCompletion(CaseContextImpl ctx, String workerName) {
    WritablePanelImpl episodic = ctx.writablePanel(ContextPanel.EPISODIC);
    List<Map<String, Object>> workers = episodic.getList("workers", Map.class); // read (lock released)
    // ← another thread reads the same list here ←
    workers = new ArrayList<>(workers);
    // ... modify workers list ...
    episodic.set("workers", workers); // write (lock acquired) — may overwrite concurrent write
}
```

`getList()` acquires and releases the read lock. The modification runs unguarded. `set()` acquires the write lock. Between the read and write, another thread calling the same method reads the same stale list and its write overwrites the first thread's increment.

### Root cause

Thread-safety of individual operations on a store does not make sequences of those operations atomic. The entire read-modify-write sequence must execute under a single lock.

### Fix

Hold the write lock for the full read-modify-write sequence using an atomic update method:

```java
// Add to the store class:
public void engineUpdate(String key, UnaryOperator<Object> updater) {
    lock.writeLock().lock();
    try {
        Object current = data.get(key);
        Object updated = updater.apply(current);
        if (updated != null) data.put(key, updated);
        else data.remove(key);
    } finally {
        lock.writeLock().unlock();
    }
}

// Caller uses the atomic update:
episodic.engineUpdate("workers", currentValue -> {
    List<Map<String, Object>> workers = currentValue instanceof List
        ? new ArrayList<>((List<Map<String, Object>>) currentValue)
        : new ArrayList<>();
    // ... modify workers ...
    return workers;
});
```

### Why this is non-obvious

Developers typically understand that individual operations on `ConcurrentHashMap` are thread-safe and assume this extends to "I read it safely, I write it safely, so the sequence is safe." The race window is invisible in unit tests because tests run sequentially by default. The bug only appears under load — Quartz worker completion callbacks, for example, can arrive concurrently for the same case and trigger simultaneous list updates.

ConcurrentHashMap.compute() serializes per-key access — inner collection needs no concurrency control

### Context
When a `ConcurrentHashMap<K, V>` holds a mutable collection as its value (e.g.
`ConcurrentHashMap<Key, List<Item>>`), the instinct is to use a thread-safe inner
collection like `CopyOnWriteArrayList` or `Collections.synchronizedList()`.

### The technique
`ConcurrentHashMap.compute()` (and `computeIfAbsent`, `merge`) already serializes
access per key — the lambda runs under the key's bin lock. Inside the lambda, the
inner collection is accessed by exactly one thread at a time. A plain `ArrayList`
is both correct and more efficient.

```java
// Wasteful — CopyOnWriteArrayList copies the entire array on every add/remove
map.compute(key, (k, entry) -> {
    var list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<Item>();  // O(n) copy per mutation
    list.add(item);
    return new Entry(list, Instant.now());
});

// Correct and efficient — ArrayList inside compute() is single-threaded
map.compute(key, (k, entry) -> {
    var list = new ArrayList<Item>();  // O(1) amortized add
    list.add(item);
    return new Entry(list, Instant.now());
});
```

### Why this is non-obvious
The reflex to use thread-safe collections inside concurrent data structures is deeply
ingrained. `CopyOnWriteArrayList` "looks right" because the outer map is concurrent.
But `compute()` already provides the synchronization — the inner thread-safe collection
adds O(n) overhead per mutation (full array copy) for zero benefit. The performance
difference is stark for eviction patterns: removing element 0 from a 500-element
`CopyOnWriteArrayList` copies 499 elements; from an `ArrayList` it shifts 499 references
in-place (still O(n) but without allocation).

### Caveat
This only applies inside `compute()`/`computeIfAbsent()`/`merge()` lambdas. If the value
is also accessed via `get()` outside these methods, the inner collection IS accessed
concurrently and DOES need its own synchronization. The pattern is safe when all mutations
go through `compute()` and reads go through `drain()` (which removes the entry atomically).

Visitor/accumulator pattern for thread-safe multi-backend aggregation — typed methods replace shared response objects

## The Technique

When assembling a response from N independent backends that run concurrently, define a typed visitor/accumulator interface rather than a shared mutable response object. Contributors call typed accumulator methods (`acc.trustScore()`, `acc.workItem()`, `acc.commitment()`) instead of returning objects to be merged. The accumulator implementation is thread-safe by construction.

```java
// Interface (stdlib types only — no domain objects)
public interface ActorStateAccumulator {
    void trustScore(Double score);               // null = unscored
    void workItem(UUID id, String title, ...);
    void commitment(UUID id, UUID channelId, ...);
}

// Thread-safe implementation
class ActorStateAccumulatorImpl implements ActorStateAccumulator {
    private final AtomicReference<Double> trustScore = new AtomicReference<>(null);
    private final CopyOnWriteArrayList<WorkItemSummary> workItems = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> warnings = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    // ... typed methods populate fields ...
}

// Aggregator runs contributors concurrently
contributors.forEach(c -> CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
    try {
        c.contribute(actorId, accumulator);
        accumulator.markSucceeded(c.sourceName());
    } catch (Exception e) {
        accumulator.markFailed(c.sourceName(), ...);
    }
}, managedExecutor));
CompletableFuture.allOf(futures).join();
return accumulator.build();
```

## Why Non-obvious

The natural approach is to make each contributor return a partial `Map<String, Object>` or a typed partial response, then merge them. This requires either synchronization on the merge step or collecting all futures before touching shared state. The visitor approach is cleaner: contributors never see each other's data; the accumulator encapsulates all thread-safety; and the interface becomes the contract for what data each backend can contribute — no `instanceof` checks, no map key strings.

## Why This Fix

The typed interface also enforces null contracts at the API level (`trustScore(Double)` accepting null is explicit; `trustScore(double)` would prevent it). The accumulator's `build()` method runs on a single thread after all contributors finish, making response assembly simple and safe.

## Constraints

Works best when contributors are truly independent (different backends, different transaction contexts). If contributors must share data with each other, the pattern breaks down.

Nested Collectors.groupingBy produces O(M) multi-key grouping without re-streaming

### Context

You need to group a flat list of items by two keys simultaneously — e.g. group attestations
by `(capabilityTag, entryId)` to produce `Map<String, Map<UUID, List<T>>>`. The naive approach
groups by the first key (O(M)), then iterates the result and re-streams the full source
collection to filter by each second key — O(N×M) total.

### What developers usually do

```java
// O(N×M): group by key1, then re-stream source per key1 group
Map<String, List<T>> byKey1 = items.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(i -> i.key1));

for (String key1 : byKey1.keySet()) {
    Map<UUID, List<T>> byKey2 = sourceList.stream()
            .filter(i -> key1.equals(i.key1))
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(i -> i.key2));
    // use byKey2...
}
```

### The technique

Use nested `Collectors.groupingBy` in a single terminal operation:

```java
// O(M): single pass produces Map<String, Map<UUID, List<T>>>
Map<String, Map<UUID, List<LedgerAttestation>>> byCapabilityAndEntry =
        actorAttestations.stream()
                .filter(a -> !CapabilityTag.GLOBAL.equals(a.capabilityTag))
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        a -> a.capabilityTag,
                        Collectors.groupingBy(a -> a.ledgerEntryId)));

// Consume directly — no secondary filtering needed
for (var capEntry : byCapabilityAndEntry.entrySet()) {
    String capabilityTag = capEntry.getKey();
    Map<UUID, List<LedgerAttestation>> capByEntry = capEntry.getValue();
    // capByEntry is already scoped to this capabilityTag — no re-filter
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The nested downstream collector `Collectors.groupingBy(a -> a.key2)` is documented but
the performance implication (one pass vs N passes) is not emphasised. Developers reaching
for "group by two keys" naturally think "group by key1, then for each group, filter and
group by key2" — which re-streams the source collection. The nested form produces both
groupings simultaneously in a single O(M) pass with no intermediate filtering.

The result type `Map<K1, Map<K2, List<T>>>` is also directly usable without reassembly —
each inner map is already the correctly-filtered `Map<K2, List<T>>` needed for the next step.

### Constraints

Works for any two-level grouping where the inner map values are lists. For three-level
nesting, add a third `Collectors.groupingBy` as the downstream of the second.

Gate optional beans in a Quarkus extension with ExcludedTypeBuildItem + @ConfigRoot(BUILD_TIME) — not @IfBuildProperty on runtime beans

### Context

A Quarkus extension (runtime + deployment modules) needs to make some `@ApplicationScoped` beans optional — present only when a config property is true at build time. JDBC-only consumers must not have CDI augmentation failures because of unsatisfied injection points in beans they'll never use.

### Technique

**In the deployment module**, declare a `@ConfigRoot(BUILD_TIME)` config interface and add a `@BuildStep` that produces `ExcludedTypeBuildItem` entries for the optional beans:

```java
// deployment/LedgerBuildTimeConfig.java
@ConfigMapping(prefix = "casehub.ledger")
@ConfigRoot(phase = ConfigPhase.BUILD_TIME)
public interface LedgerBuildTimeConfig {
    ReactiveConfig reactive();
    interface ReactiveConfig {
        @WithDefault("false")
        boolean enabled();
    }
}

// deployment/LedgerProcessor.java
@BuildStep
void excludeReactiveBeans(
        LedgerBuildTimeConfig config,
        BuildProducer<ExcludedTypeBuildItem> excluded) {
    if (!config.reactive().enabled()) {
        excluded.produce(new ExcludedTypeBuildItem(
            ReactiveKeyRotationService.class.getName()));
        excluded.produce(new ExcludedTypeBuildItem(
            ReactiveLedgerVerificationService.class.getName()));
    }
}
```

**In `application.properties`** (or test resources):
```properties
# Reactive-enabled consumers and test suites
casehub.ledger.reactive.enabled=true

# JDBC-only consumers: leave unset (defaults to false)
# → ExcludedTypeBuildItem removes beans at augmentation
# → No unsatisfied dependency failure
```

**Also declare the property in the runtime `@ConfigMapping`** to suppress `SRCFG00050` (see related gotcha GE-20260519-098413). Use a warning Javadoc that the runtime accessor is not the authoritative gate.

The optional beans carry no gating annotation — exclusion is entirely the deployment module's concern. This is the canonical Quarkus extension pattern; `@IfBuildProperty` on runtime beans is for application beans, not extension-provided beans.

### Why this is non-obvious

Most Quarkus documentation and tutorials show `@IfBuildProperty` as the conditional bean mechanism. But for extension-provided beans, the correct pattern is `ExcludedTypeBuildItem` from the deployment `@BuildStep`. The distinction — `@IfBuildProperty` is for app beans, `ExcludedTypeBuildItem` is for extension beans — is not prominently documented. The failure mode (beans appear or disappear unexpectedly, or `SRCFG00050` at startup) is subtle enough that developers often reach for `Instance<T>` workarounds instead.

**See also:** [GE-20260519-098413](GE-20260519-098413.md) — @ConfigRoot(BUILD_TIME) in deployment module doesn't suppress SRCFG00050 when runtime @ConfigMapping owns same prefix | [GE-20260512-b3f32a](GE-20260512-b3f32a.md) — @IfBuildProperty/@UnlessBuildProperty evaluated at augmentation only

**See also:** [GE-20260520-c52767](GE-20260520-c52767.md) — BUILD_TIME @ConfigRoot properties in application.properties cause SRCFG00050

