The entity does have `@Entity`. The error message is completely misleading.

## Context

Occurs when a repository class implements `PanacheRepository<Entity, Id>` (or `PanacheRepositoryBase<Entity, Id>`) but the entity is a plain JPA `@Entity` POJO that does **not** extend `PanacheEntityBase` or `PanacheEntity`.

## Root Cause

Quarkus Panache's bytecode enhancement for repository methods (the automatic method override mentioned in the error) is only applied to repositories whose entity type went through Panache's own build-time processor. That processor only recognises entities that extend `PanacheEntityBase`. A plain `@Entity` is never processed by Panache's build step, so the repository methods are never enhanced — the stubs remain and throw at runtime.

## Fix

Do **not** use `PanacheRepository<PlainEntity, Id>` when the entity is a plain JPA POJO. Instead, inject `EntityManager` directly and use JPQL:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
@Alternative
public class JpaLedgerEntryRepository implements LedgerEntryRepository {

@Inject
    EntityManager em;

@Override
    public List<LedgerEntry> findBySubjectId(UUID subjectId) {
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT e FROM LedgerEntry e WHERE e.subjectId = :id ORDER BY e.sequenceNumber ASC",
            LedgerEntry.class)
            .setParameter("id", subjectId)
            .getResultList();
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

The Quarkus docs describe `PanacheRepository<Entity, Id>` as a "repository pattern" suitable for any JPA entity — distinct from the active-record pattern that requires `PanacheEntityBase`. The docs don't mention that the entity must still be processed by Panache's build step. The error message says "did you forget to annotate with @Entity" which actively misleads — the entity has `@Entity` and that's not the problem.

**See also:** GE-20260420-b9259e (LedgerAttestation plain @Entity — Panache statics cause compile error)

Static Panache entity calls bypass injected store tenant filtering

### Symptom

A multi-tenant query returns data from all tenants instead of just the caller's tenant. The store-level filtering works correctly in most places, but specific code paths return unscoped results.

### Context

Application-level multi-tenancy implemented via CDI store interfaces that inject `CurrentPrincipal` and add `tenancyId` to WHERE clauses. Hibernate Panache entities also have static convenience methods (`Entity.list()`, `Entity.find()`, `Entity.findById()`).

### What was tried (didn't work)

Audited the store implementations — all correctly filter by tenancyId. The leak was in code that bypassed the store entirely.

### Root cause

Panache static methods like `WorkItem.list("parentId", parentId)` execute directly against the entity manager without going through the injected store. They have no access to the request-scoped `CurrentPrincipal` and no tenant filtering. Any code using static Panache calls in a multi-tenant app is executing unscoped queries.

### Fix

Replace every static Panache call with an equivalent store method that includes tenant filtering:

```java
// WRONG — bypasses tenant filtering
List<WorkItem> children = WorkItem.list("parentId", parentId);

// RIGHT — store method includes tenancyId in WHERE clause
List<WorkItem> children = workItemStore.findByParentId(parentId);
```

Add the missing store methods:

```java
// Store interface — default method for non-JPA backends
default List<WorkItem> findByParentId(UUID parentId) {
    return scanAll().stream()
        .filter(w -> parentId.equals(w.getParentId()))
        .toList();
}

// JPA implementation — tenant-scoped query
@Override
public List<WorkItem> findByParentId(UUID parentId) {
    return list("parentId = ?1 and tenancyId = ?2",
        parentId, currentPrincipal.tenancyId());
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

Panache's static methods are the documented, idiomatic way to query entities in Quarkus. They appear in tutorials, quickstarts, and migration guides. Nothing warns that they become an unscoped data leak when combined with application-level multi-tenancy. The static API looks identical to the instance API but has fundamentally different scoping behaviour.

### Alternatives considered

- **Hibernate @Filter with @FilterDef**: adds tenant filtering at the entity level, but requires session-level filter activation which has its own lifecycle issues
- **PostgreSQL Row-Level Security**: defence-in-depth layer planned separately (#257) but doesn't replace application-level filtering for non-Postgres backends
- **Ban Panache static imports via linting**: catches new violations but doesn't fix existing ones; chosen as a complementary measure

**See also:** [GE-20260530-4387cb](GE-20260530-4387cb.md) — casehub-qhorus MessageService Panache static calls bypass InMemory store in @QuarkusTest | [GE-20260512-66d997](GE-20260512-66d997.md) — Panache static methods bypass CDI @Alternative stores

Panache PanacheEntityBase.persist(Iterable<T>) batch-persists in one Hibernate session flush — enables JDBC batch mode

## Context

Overriding a blocking SPI method (e.g. `storeAll(List<MemoryInput>)`) to persist N JPA
entities atomically in a single transaction with maximum JDBC efficiency.

## Technique

Build all entities first (using `stream().toList()` or any eager collection), then call
`MemoryEntry.persist(entries)` once with the complete `Iterable<MemoryEntry>`:

```java
@Override
@Transactional(TxType.REQUIRED)
public List<String> storeAll(List<MemoryInput> inputs) {
    if (inputs.isEmpty()) return List.of();
    var entries = inputs.stream().map(input -> {
        MemoryEntry e = new MemoryEntry();
        e.memoryId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        // ... populate fields
        return e;
    }).toList();

MemoryEntry.persist(entries);   // ← single flush, all N entities

return entries.stream().map(e -> e.memoryId).toList();
}
```

Configure JDBC batching in `application.properties`:
```properties
quarkus.hibernate-orm.jdbc.statement-batch-size=50
```

Panache delegates `persist(Iterable)` to `em.persist()` for each entity within the
same Hibernate session flush. With `statement-batch-size` configured, Hibernate groups
the resulting `INSERT` statements into a single batched `PreparedStatement`, reducing
N DB round-trips to ⌈N / batch-size⌉.

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

Developers reach for `entity.persist()` in a loop inside `@Transactional` — this IS
atomic (single commit) but does NOT enable JDBC batch mode. Hibernate issues individual
`INSERT` statements regardless of `statement-batch-size` when `em.persist()` is called
one entity at a time.

`persist(Iterable)` presents all entities to Hibernate before the flush, allowing the
batch assembler to group them. The Panache overload exists and works, but is easy to
miss because the common pattern is always the single-entity form.

## Bonus: fail-fast semantics

Building the entity list eagerly (`.toList()`) before calling `persist(Iterable)` means
any validation or security check inside the stream (e.g. `assertTenant()`) throws before
any entity reaches the persistence layer. Combined with `@Transactional(REQUIRED)`, this
gives all-or-nothing batch semantics: either all N entities are committed or none are.

@TestTransaction prevents cross-test message leakage when gateway inbound persists messages inside @QuarkusTest

## Context

A `@QuarkusTest` calls `gateway.receiveHumanMessage(ref, raw)` which internally calls `messageService.send()` — a `@Transactional` method that persists a message. Without transaction rollback, that message persists across tests and appears in subsequent tests' `checkMessages()` / full-text search results.

## The Problem

```java
// Test A (no @TestTransaction):
gateway.receiveHumanMessage(ref,
    new InboundHumanMessage("user-42", "Can you stop the analysis?", ...));
// Message committed to DB — visible to ALL subsequent tests

// Test B (unrelated):
var results = tools.checkMessages("some-channel", "observer", ...);
// Sees "Can you stop the analysis?" — unexpected match in artefact search for "analysis"
```

This is subtle because the leaking test looks like a read-only test (it only calls `receiveHumanMessage`) but actually triggers a persistent write via the gateway inbound path.

## Fix

Annotate gateway inbound tests with `@TestTransaction` to roll back after each test:

```java
@Test
@TestTransaction
void receiveHumanMessage_createsMessageWithHumanSender() {
    var ch = tools.listChannels().stream()
            .filter(c -> "test-ch".equals(c.name())).findFirst().orElseThrow();
    gateway.receiveHumanMessage(new ChannelRef(ch.channelId(), "test-ch"),
            new InboundHumanMessage("user-42", "Can you stop?", Instant.now(), Map.of()));

var messages = tools.checkMessages("test-ch", "observer", ...);
    assertTrue(messages.messages().stream()
            .anyMatch(m -> "human:user-42".equals(m.sender())));
    // Rolled back after test — no leakage
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

Tests that only invoke gateway methods look like they don't write to the DB. The persistence happens inside `MessageService.send()` which is called internally by the gateway — invisible to someone reading the test. Standard `@QuarkusTest` isolation doesn't roll back CDI-managed transactions unless `@TestTransaction` is explicitly present. The failure mode is a distant test failure with no obvious connection to the inbound test.

@Alternative on a CDI bean silently suppresses all @DefaultBean beans of the same type — they vanish from the container entirely

### Symptom

`Instance<ChatModel>` iteration returns only the `@Alternative` bean. The `@DefaultBean` ChatModel (registered by quarkus-langchain4j via `SyntheticBeanBuildItem.defaultBean()`) is completely absent from the container — not deprioritized, gone.

### Context

A platform module needed to provide a `ChatModel` adapter (`@Alternative @Priority(10)`) that wraps an `AgentProvider` as a `ChatModel`. The same deployment also has quarkus-langchain4j providing a raw `ChatModel` as `@DefaultBean`. A second bean uses `Instance<ChatModel>` to find the raw model — but after filtering out the adapter, nothing remains.

### What was tried (didn't work)

`@Alternative @Priority(10) @ApplicationScoped` on the adapter bean. Expected: both the adapter and the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel visible in `Instance<ChatModel>`, with `@Priority` disambiguating for direct injection. Actual: the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel vanishes entirely.

### Root cause

ArC's `@DefaultBean` semantics state: *"the bean will only be used if no other bean of this type is configured."* An `@Alternative` with `@Priority` counts as "another bean" — it suppresses the `@DefaultBean` entirely, removing it from the container. This is not priority ordering — it is total suppression.

### Fix

Use `@DefaultBean @Priority(10)` instead of `@Alternative @Priority(10)`.

```java
// WRONG — suppresses quarkus-langchain4j's @DefaultBean ChatModel
@Alternative
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }

// RIGHT — coexists with other @DefaultBean ChatModel beans
@DefaultBean
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }
```

Two `@DefaultBean` beans coexist — neither suppresses the other. `@Priority(10)` wins for direct `@Inject ChatModel` injection. `Instance<ChatModel>` sees both, enabling runtime filtering.

A consumer-provided non-`@DefaultBean` ChatModel properly overrides both defaults — the `@DefaultBean` suppression rule works as expected when a "real" bean is present.

### Why this is non-obvious

Developers expect `@Alternative` to win the injection point but leave other beans in the container for `Instance` lookup. The mental model is "priority ordering among all candidates" when the reality is "total removal of @DefaultBean beans when any non-default bean exists."

The ArC javadoc confirms: *"Default beans can optionally declare @Priority. Priority value is used for bean ordering and during typesafe resolution to disambiguate multiple matching default beans."* This `@DefaultBean @Priority` pattern is the correct way to have two platform-provided defaults with deterministic selection.

### Why this fix

`@DefaultBean @Priority` is architecturally correct — both beans are "defaults" that yield to consumer-provided beans. `@Alternative` implies the bean is an override, which is semantically wrong when both beans are platform infrastructure that should coexist.

### Alternatives considered

- **CDI qualifiers** (`@Named`, custom qualifier) — would work but forces callers to qualify injection points. Breaks the "just add to classpath" activation model.
- **Conditional activation** (`@IfBuildProperty`) — too rigid; the coexistence scenario is legitimate in deployments using both adapters.

**See also:** GE-20260601-fcf0d9 — two @DefaultBean beans for the same type give AmbiguousResolutionException, not Unsatisfied | GE-20260623-c651a1 — removing @Alternative from one bean in a multi-implementation chain silently changes resolution

Config-driven @Produces @DefaultBean for engine-internal strategy selection with consumer override

### The technique

When a module ships multiple implementations of an interface (e.g. `NoOpStrategy`, `TopLevelStrategy`, `JsonPatchStrategy`) and needs to select one via config while still allowing consumers to override entirely, use a config-driven `@Produces @DefaultBean` producer instead of CDI annotation games (`@Alternative`, `@Priority`, `@DefaultBean` on individual classes).

### Pattern

```java
@ApplicationScoped
class StrategyProducer {

@ConfigProperty(name = "mymodule.strategy", defaultValue = "none")
    String strategy;   // package-private for direct test access

@Produces
    @DefaultBean
    @ApplicationScoped
    MyStrategy produce() {
        return switch (strategy) {
            case "none"       -> new NoOpStrategy();
            case "top-level"  -> new TopLevelStrategy();
            case "json-patch" -> new JsonPatchStrategy();
            default -> throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Unknown mymodule.strategy: '" + strategy +
                "'. Valid values: none, top-level, json-patch");
        };
    }
}
```

The three strategy classes carry **no CDI annotations** — they are plain POJOs instantiated by the producer.

### Key properties

- **Consumer override:** because the produced bean is `@DefaultBean`, any consumer-provided `@ApplicationScoped MyStrategy` wins automatically with no configuration.
- **Config selection:** `@ConfigProperty` with a `defaultValue` means the producer is always safe to instantiate even when the consumer has overridden the bean (the `produce()` method is never called in that case).
- **Fail-fast:** unknown config values throw `IllegalStateException` at boot — include the config key name and bad value in the message.
- **Testability:** the `strategy` field is package-private, allowing plain JUnit tests to set it directly without Quarkus or Mockito.

### When to use

Use this pattern when: a library or platform module ships several bundled implementations of an interface; only one can be active at a time; the choice belongs to config (not classpath composition); and external consumers should be able to replace the whole thing with their own implementation.

Do **not** use this for SPIs that consumers are expected to extend — use `@DefaultBean` directly on the no-op class for those (the standard consumer-replaceable SPI pattern).

### Why non-obvious

The instinct is to use `@Alternative` / `selected-alternatives` or `@Priority` on the individual strategy classes. Those approaches either require explicit config per alternative, create CDI ambiguity between bundled implementations, or make all implementations always-on. The producer pattern cleanly separates "which one" (config) from "how CDI wires it" (producer), and the strategy classes stay as plain testable POJOs.

