CamelCase compound preservation in BM25 tokenization — exact identifier matches outrank partial via IDF

### Symptom

BM25 keyword search over code-domain text fails to distinguish exact identifier matches from coincidental word matches. Searching for `ChatModel` ranks documents containing unrelated "chat" and "model" equally to documents about the `ChatModel` Java interface.

### Context

Standard BM25 tokenizers (word-boundary split + lowercase) produce `chat` and `model` from `ChatModel`. A document about "a chat room model" matches identically to one about `dev.langchain4j.model.chat.ChatModel`. The compound identifier is destroyed.

### Fix

Produce both the compound form AND the components during tokenization:

```
ChatModel → [chatmodel, chat, model]
DefaultBean → [defaultbean, default, bean]
HTTPClient → [httpclient, http, client]
```

The compound token `chatmodel` is rare (high IDF) — it appears only in documents that actually discuss `ChatModel`. The component tokens `chat` and `model` are common (low IDF). BM25's IDF weighting naturally ranks documents matching the rare compound higher than documents matching only the common components.

```java
static List<String> tokenize(String text) {
    List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();
    String[] segments = text.split("[^a-zA-Z0-9]+");
    for (String segment : segments) {
        if (segment.isEmpty()) continue;
        List<String> parts = splitCamelCase(segment);
        for (String part : parts) tokens.add(part.toLowerCase());
        if (parts.size() > 1) tokens.add(segment.toLowerCase());
    }
    return tokens;
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The standard approach to CamelCase tokenization is to split into components only — `ChatModel → [chat, model]`. Keeping the compound alongside the components seems redundant until you realize IDF does the ranking work: the compound is always rarer than its components, so BM25 automatically favors exact matches without any special scoring logic. The insight is that the tokenizer controls ranking quality through IDF statistics, not through scoring formulas.

Uni.createFrom().emitter() is wrong for one-shot callback bridging — use completionStage() + CompletableFuture.orTimeout()

## Symptom

Wrapping a streaming callback API into `Uni<T>` using `Uni.createFrom().emitter()` appears to work — but the Uni has no built-in timeout, the risk of calling `emitter.complete()` in both `onCompleteResponse` and `onError` exists, and `emitter()` semantics are for push streams, not one-shot results.

## Context

When bridging a callback-based async API (e.g. `StreamingChatModel.chat(request, handler)` from LangChain4j) into a Mutiny `Uni<T>`, `emitter()` looks like the natural choice. But it has three problems for one-shot use: (1) no built-in timeout — a hung provider hangs the Uni indefinitely, (2) it is designed for push streams where `emit()` may be called many times, (3) both `onCompleteResponse` and `onError` callbacks could call `emitter.complete()` by mistake, causing IllegalStateException.

## Root Cause

`Uni.createFrom().emitter()` is a push-stream primitive. For a one-shot async result (exactly one completion or error), the correct primitive is `CompletableFuture` — which has timeout support via `orTimeout()`, clear single-completion semantics, and maps cleanly to Mutiny via `Uni.createFrom().completionStage()`.

## Fix

```java
CompletableFuture<Optional<T>> future = new CompletableFuture<>();
llm.chat(request, new StreamingChatResponseHandler() {
    @Override
    public void onCompleteResponse(ChatResponse response) {
        future.complete(parseOrEmpty(response.aiMessage().text()));
    }
    @Override
    public void onError(Throwable error) {
        future.completeExceptionally(error);  // not future.complete(empty) — let onFailure handle it
    }
});
return Uni.createFrom().completionStage(
        future.orTimeout(TIMEOUT_SECONDS, TimeUnit.SECONDS))
    .onFailure().recoverWithItem(e -> {
        log.warn("Streaming LLM failed (" + e.getMessage() + "), falling back");
        return Optional.empty();
    });
```

`onError` calls `completeExceptionally` — the `onFailure().recoverWithItem()` in Mutiny handles both timeout and LLM errors uniformly, and the fallback is explicit at the Mutiny level.

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

`emitter()` is the most prominent `Uni.createFrom()` variant for async work and the name sounds right for a callback-based API. The problems (no timeout, push-stream semantics, double-complete risk) only surface under failure conditions — in the happy path, `emitter()` and `completionStage()` behave identically.

Use Instance<T> for optional CDI injection — resolves gracefully to null when no bean exists

## Context

A library module needs to use a CDI bean (e.g. `EmbeddingModel`) that may or may not be present in the consuming application's classpath. Direct `@Inject EmbeddingModel` would fail with `UnsatisfiedResolutionException` at CDI startup when no provider is configured.

## Technique

Inject `Instance<T>` instead of `T` directly. `Instance<T>` is always satisfiable — CDI never fails to inject it. Call `instance.isResolvable()` at use time to check whether a bean actually exists:

```java
@Inject
public EmbeddingSkillMatcher(final Instance<EmbeddingModel> modelInstance) {
    this.model = modelInstance.isResolvable() ? modelInstance.get() : null;
}
```

At call time, check and degrade gracefully:

```java
public double score(SkillProfile profile, SelectionContext ctx) {
    if (model == null) {
        LOG.warn("No EmbeddingModel available — returning -1.0");
        return -1.0;
    }
    // ... call model
}
```

For unit tests, inject a concrete `EmbeddingModel` directly via a package-private test constructor — no `Instance<>` wrapper needed:

```java
// Test constructor — bypasses CDI
EmbeddingSkillMatcher(final EmbeddingModel model) {
    this.model = model;
}
```

## Why this is non-obvious

Most developers either:
1. Use direct `@Inject T` (fails at boot when bean is absent)
2. Use `@Inject @Default T` with a no-op default bean (pollutes CDI context)
3. Wrap everything in `@IfBuildProperty` conditions (ties optional behaviour to build time)

`Instance<T>` solves this purely at runtime, with no build-time conditions and no extra beans. The pattern is documented in the CDI spec but rarely seen in practice because the common case is non-optional injection.

### Why this fix

`Instance<T>` is the CDI-native solution for optional dependencies. Alternatives (no-op default beans, build-time conditions) add complexity and make testing harder. `Instance<T>` keeps the code clean and the library usable in both AI-enabled and AI-disabled contexts.

**See also:** GE-20260422-f922f3 — quarkus-langchain4j-core stalls @QuarkusTest augmentation when no model provider configured

@Startup @ApplicationScoped prevents blocking @PostConstruct from running on Vert.x IO thread

# @Startup @ApplicationScoped prevents blocking @PostConstruct from running on Vert.x IO thread

## The technique

Add `@Startup` to any `@ApplicationScoped` bean whose `@PostConstruct` makes a blocking call:

```java
@Startup           // forces eager init on main thread during boot
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyClient {

@PostConstruct
    void validate() {
        // blocking: process probe, network check, file I/O, etc.
        new ProcessBuilder("claude", "--version").start().waitFor(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

Without `@Startup`, Quarkus ARC initializes `@ApplicationScoped` beans **lazily** — on first injection. If the first injection happens from a reactive handler (inside a Vert.x event-loop callback, a REST endpoint, a CDI observer), the `@PostConstruct` runs on the Vert.x IO thread. Any blocking call there violates the non-blocking contract and either deadlocks under load or triggers a `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException`.

`@Startup` forces initialization during application boot on the main thread, before any reactive handlers are active.

## Why this is non-obvious

`@Startup` is documented as "run initialization at startup" but the docs frame it in terms of *when* (boot vs. first use), not *which thread*. The connection to IO-thread safety is not mentioned. Developers who know about `@Startup` typically use it for "I want this to run at boot time" — not as a reactive-safety mechanism.

The failure mode is intermittent: `@PostConstruct` is safe when the first injection happens from a non-reactive context (tests, CLI, HTTP request on a worker thread). It only fails when the first injection happens under reactive load — making it a production-only bug in many setups.

## Codebase pattern

Same pattern used by `PathParserConfigurator` and `MongoPreferenceIndexes` in casehub-platform. Canonical for any bean that initialises eagerly and blocks during `@PostConstruct`.

## Why non-obvious

The standard mental model for `@ApplicationScoped` is "singleton, available everywhere, initialized once." The lazy-init detail — and its thread-safety implications in a reactive runtime — is not in the CDI spec, only in Quarkus ARC internals. A developer migrating from Spring (`@Bean` methods always run on the startup thread) would have no reason to anticipate the problem.

**See also:** [GE-20260522-daca26](GE-20260522-daca26.md) — Mutiny MultiEmitter.emit() from Vert.x I/O thread silently drops SSE frames | [GE-20260505-da346d](GE-20260505-da346d.md) — @ApplicationScoped CDI beans are always-active in Quarkus

**See also:** [GE-20260605-e42cc5](GE-20260605-e42cc5.md) — @Blocking on plain @ApplicationScoped method causes Quarkus test classloader failure

**See also:** [GE-20260607-cedf69](GE-20260607-cedf69.md) — @Startup @ApplicationScoped does not auto-invoke arbitrary methods — requires @PostConstruct

**See also:** [GE-20260615-d065bf](GE-20260615-d065bf.md) — two @Startup @ApplicationScoped beans have no guaranteed initialization order

CDI container wiring vs service-loader wiring in large JVM frameworks

## Pattern

Large JVM frameworks that expose extension points make a foundational choice: wire extensions via **CDI container injection** or via **service-loader / explicit registration**. Both achieve the same structural depth (thousands of interfaces, deep abstraction hierarchies) but differ radically in how dependencies flow at runtime.

### CDI container wiring (Quarkus style)

Extensions are beans. Callers inject them via `@Inject`, `Instance<T>`, or `@Any Instance<T>`. The CDI container owns lifetime and resolution.

```java
// Extension point
public interface ExpiryPolicy {
    String name();
    boolean isExpired(Session s, Duration timeout);
}

// Discovery — zero explicit registration
@ApplicationScoped
public class PolicyRegistry {
    @Inject @Any Instance<ExpiryPolicy> policies;

public ExpiryPolicy get(String name) {
        return policies.stream()
            .filter(p -> p.name().equals(name))
            .findFirst().orElseThrow();
    }
}
```

### Service-loader wiring (Hibernate style)

Extensions are registered via `META-INF/services/<InterfaceFQN>` files or explicit `ServiceRegistry` calls. The framework owns resolution; no CDI container required.

```java
// META-INF/services/org.hibernate.integrator.spi.Integrator
// → com.example.MyIntegrator

// Discovery — explicit service lookup
ServiceLoader<Integrator> loader =
    ServiceLoader.load(Integrator.class, classLoader);
```

## Why this is non-obvious

The two approaches look structurally identical at a coarse level — same number of interfaces, same abstraction depth ratio. A structural fingerprint alone won't distinguish them. The signal is in the **injection-to-file ratio**: CDI-wired frameworks approach 0.4 injections/file; service-loader frameworks stay near 0.002. That gap (200×) survives normalisation and is the discriminator.

This matters when **integrating the two styles**: a Hibernate extension brought into a Quarkus application must be wrapped in a CDI producer or adapter bean — the wiring strategies don't compose directly.

### Alternatives considered

- **Manual factory / registry**: explicit `Map<String, Extension>` maintained in code. Works, but requires every extension author to register manually. Fragile at scale.
- **Reflection-based scanning**: scan classpath for implementations. Non-deterministic ordering, classloading hazards, slow startup.
- **Both at once (hybrid)**: use service-loader for bootstrap discovery, CDI for runtime injection. Adds complexity; only warranted when a framework must work both inside and outside a CDI container.

CDI @PostConstruct JPA calls fail silently on Vert.x IO thread when engine's registerKnownDefinitions triggers lazy bean init

### Symptom

Quarkus startup fails with `BlockingNotAllowedException` or `ContextNotActiveException` from a `@PostConstruct` method that calls `ChannelService.findByName()` (JPA/Panache). The stack trace shows the call originates from `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry.registerKnownDefinitions()` running on a Vert.x IO thread.

### Context

When `QuarkMindCaseHub extends CaseHub` is discovered by CDI, the engine's `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry` calls `registerKnownDefinitions()` on a Vert.x IO thread during startup event processing. This triggers lazy resolution of CDI beans injected by the CaseHub subclass (e.g. `Instance<TaskDefinition>`). If any of those beans have `@PostConstruct` methods that perform blocking JPA work (channel creation, preference loading), the call hits the Vert.x IO thread blocker.

### Root cause

Vert.x IO threads prohibit blocking operations. JPA/Panache calls are blocking. The engine's case definition registration happens on an IO thread, which transitively constructs `@ApplicationScoped` beans that were designed to initialize eagerly via `@PostConstruct`.

### Fix

Defer blocking work from `@PostConstruct` to lazy initialization with double-checked locking:

```java
// Before (fails on IO thread):
@PostConstruct
void init() {
    channel = channelService.findByName("my-channel")
        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...));
}

// After (lazy, thread-safe):
private volatile UUID channelId;

private void ensureChannel() {
    if (channelId == null) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (channelId == null) {
                channelId = QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()
                    .call(() -> channelService.findByName("my-channel")
                        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...)))
                    .id();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Call `ensureChannel()` on first use (e.g. first `execute()` call) instead of in `@PostConstruct`.

### Why this is non-obvious

The `@PostConstruct` pattern is standard for CDI bean initialization. It works for all non-JPA work. The failure only occurs when the bean is transitively discovered by the engine's CaseDefinitionRegistry — a dependency chain that crosses module boundaries and isn't visible from the bean's own code.

@Alternative on a CDI bean silently suppresses all @DefaultBean beans of the same type — they vanish from the container entirely

### Symptom

`Instance<ChatModel>` iteration returns only the `@Alternative` bean. The `@DefaultBean` ChatModel (registered by quarkus-langchain4j via `SyntheticBeanBuildItem.defaultBean()`) is completely absent from the container — not deprioritized, gone.

### Context

A platform module needed to provide a `ChatModel` adapter (`@Alternative @Priority(10)`) that wraps an `AgentProvider` as a `ChatModel`. The same deployment also has quarkus-langchain4j providing a raw `ChatModel` as `@DefaultBean`. A second bean uses `Instance<ChatModel>` to find the raw model — but after filtering out the adapter, nothing remains.

### What was tried (didn't work)

`@Alternative @Priority(10) @ApplicationScoped` on the adapter bean. Expected: both the adapter and the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel visible in `Instance<ChatModel>`, with `@Priority` disambiguating for direct injection. Actual: the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel vanishes entirely.

### Root cause

ArC's `@DefaultBean` semantics state: *"the bean will only be used if no other bean of this type is configured."* An `@Alternative` with `@Priority` counts as "another bean" — it suppresses the `@DefaultBean` entirely, removing it from the container. This is not priority ordering — it is total suppression.

### Fix

Use `@DefaultBean @Priority(10)` instead of `@Alternative @Priority(10)`.

```java
// WRONG — suppresses quarkus-langchain4j's @DefaultBean ChatModel
@Alternative
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }

// RIGHT — coexists with other @DefaultBean ChatModel beans
@DefaultBean
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }
```

Two `@DefaultBean` beans coexist — neither suppresses the other. `@Priority(10)` wins for direct `@Inject ChatModel` injection. `Instance<ChatModel>` sees both, enabling runtime filtering.

A consumer-provided non-`@DefaultBean` ChatModel properly overrides both defaults — the `@DefaultBean` suppression rule works as expected when a "real" bean is present.

### Why this is non-obvious

Developers expect `@Alternative` to win the injection point but leave other beans in the container for `Instance` lookup. The mental model is "priority ordering among all candidates" when the reality is "total removal of @DefaultBean beans when any non-default bean exists."

The ArC javadoc confirms: *"Default beans can optionally declare @Priority. Priority value is used for bean ordering and during typesafe resolution to disambiguate multiple matching default beans."* This `@DefaultBean @Priority` pattern is the correct way to have two platform-provided defaults with deterministic selection.

### Why this fix

`@DefaultBean @Priority` is architecturally correct — both beans are "defaults" that yield to consumer-provided beans. `@Alternative` implies the bean is an override, which is semantically wrong when both beans are platform infrastructure that should coexist.

### Alternatives considered

- **CDI qualifiers** (`@Named`, custom qualifier) — would work but forces callers to qualify injection points. Breaks the "just add to classpath" activation model.
- **Conditional activation** (`@IfBuildProperty`) — too rigid; the coexistence scenario is legitimate in deployments using both adapters.

**See also:** GE-20260601-fcf0d9 — two @DefaultBean beans for the same type give AmbiguousResolutionException, not Unsatisfied | GE-20260623-c651a1 — removing @Alternative from one bean in a multi-implementation chain silently changes resolution

@Transactional on @PostConstruct is silently ignored — Arc proxy not created yet

### Symptom

A `@PostConstruct` method calls an injected service method annotated with `@Transactional`. At runtime:
- No error or exception is thrown
- The transaction is silently never started
- Database operations inside the service method fail with "no EntityManager associated" or run outside a transaction, silently committing or rolling back contrary to expectations
- Annotating `@PostConstruct` itself with `@Transactional` also has no effect

### Context

Occurs in any Quarkus `@ApplicationScoped` (or other normal-scoped) CDI bean whose `@PostConstruct` method invokes a service that requires a JTA transaction. Classic example: bootstrapping a channel or configuration record in a qhorus `ChannelService.create()` call during bean initialisation.

### Root Cause

Quarkus CDI (`Arc`) implements `@Transactional` via a CDI proxy subclass. When `@PostConstruct` executes, the proxy has not yet been created — the bean is being initialised. The call is made directly on the concrete instance, bypassing the proxy entirely. The `@Transactional` interceptor never fires.

This is not a bug in Arc: the CDI specification requires `@PostConstruct` to execute on the bean instance, not through the proxy. `@Transactional` only works through proxy dispatch.

### Fix

Wrap the transactional work in `QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()`:

```java
import io.quarkus.narayana.jta.QuarkusTransaction;

@PostConstruct
void init() {
    QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew().call(() -> {
        channelService.findByName(CHANNEL_NAME)
            .map(c -> c.id)
            .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...).id);
        return null;
    });
    // Other non-transactional init...
    activeTypes = computeActiveTypes(consumers);
}
```

`QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()` starts a real JTA transaction programmatically on the current thread. The transaction commits (or rolls back on exception) when the lambda returns. This does not require a proxy.

### Why this is non-obvious

Developers naturally annotate `@PostConstruct` with `@Transactional` (it compiles fine), or rely on injected services being `@Transactional` to "just work" when called from `@PostConstruct`. Neither approach produces any error — the annotation is present, the service is injected, the method call executes — but the transaction silently never starts. The symptom only surfaces as unexpected database behaviour (missing data, constraint violations, or EntityManager-not-bound errors) rather than a clear "no transaction" message.

### Why this fix

`QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()` is the programmatic API specifically designed for contexts where CDI interception is unavailable — tests, listeners, and lifecycle methods. It is unambiguous about starting a new transaction and does not depend on proxy dispatch.

### Alternatives considered

- Annotating `@PostConstruct` with `@Transactional` — no effect; proxy not created yet
- Self-injecting the bean via `@Inject Instance<Self>` and calling through the proxy — works but awkward and misleading in an init method
- Moving init logic to a `@Observes StartupEvent` method — works and the proxy IS active at that point; valid alternative but changes when init runs (after full CDI startup vs during bean creation)

**See also:** [GE-20260512-a9ad9f](GE-20260512-a9ad9f.md) — raw ExecutorService drops CDI context — @Transactional silently broken on background threads | [GE-20260513-a49d06](GE-20260513-a49d06.md) — CDI this.method() call bypasses @Transactional proxy | [GE-20260501-884024](../quarkus/GE-20260501-884024.md) — @Observes(AFTER_SUCCESS) doesn't fire when no JTA transaction

**See also:** [GE-20260518-069f64](GE-20260518-069f64.md) — calling @Transactional method via this inside Mutiny lambda silently bypasses CDI proxy

