CDI @PostConstruct JPA calls fail silently on Vert.x IO thread when engine's registerKnownDefinitions triggers lazy bean init

### Symptom

Quarkus startup fails with `BlockingNotAllowedException` or `ContextNotActiveException` from a `@PostConstruct` method that calls `ChannelService.findByName()` (JPA/Panache). The stack trace shows the call originates from `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry.registerKnownDefinitions()` running on a Vert.x IO thread.

### Context

When `QuarkMindCaseHub extends CaseHub` is discovered by CDI, the engine's `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry` calls `registerKnownDefinitions()` on a Vert.x IO thread during startup event processing. This triggers lazy resolution of CDI beans injected by the CaseHub subclass (e.g. `Instance<TaskDefinition>`). If any of those beans have `@PostConstruct` methods that perform blocking JPA work (channel creation, preference loading), the call hits the Vert.x IO thread blocker.

### Root cause

Vert.x IO threads prohibit blocking operations. JPA/Panache calls are blocking. The engine's case definition registration happens on an IO thread, which transitively constructs `@ApplicationScoped` beans that were designed to initialize eagerly via `@PostConstruct`.

### Fix

Defer blocking work from `@PostConstruct` to lazy initialization with double-checked locking:

```java
// Before (fails on IO thread):
@PostConstruct
void init() {
    channel = channelService.findByName("my-channel")
        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...));
}

// After (lazy, thread-safe):
private volatile UUID channelId;

private void ensureChannel() {
    if (channelId == null) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (channelId == null) {
                channelId = QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()
                    .call(() -> channelService.findByName("my-channel")
                        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...)))
                    .id();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Call `ensureChannel()` on first use (e.g. first `execute()` call) instead of in `@PostConstruct`.

### Why this is non-obvious

The `@PostConstruct` pattern is standard for CDI bean initialization. It works for all non-JPA work. The failure only occurs when the bean is transitively discovered by the engine's CaseDefinitionRegistry — a dependency chain that crosses module boundaries and isn't visible from the bean's own code.

@Startup @ApplicationScoped + @PostConstruct is the correct pattern for idempotent MongoDB index creation in Quarkus

### Context
JPA with Flyway handles schema migrations (including indexes) declaratively. MongoDB has
no equivalent mechanism in Quarkus — there is no migration runner, no `migrate-at-start`,
no DDL auto-generation. Indexes must be created explicitly before they are needed.
Without a canonical pattern, developers either create indexes out-of-band (fragile, not
reproducible) or reach for `@Observer(StartupEvent.class)` (works but verbose).

### Technique
Use `@Startup @ApplicationScoped` with a `@PostConstruct` method. `@Startup` forces
eager bean initialization at application startup before any request is processed.
`@PostConstruct` runs the index creation once. MongoDB's `createIndex` is idempotent —
calling it on an already-existing index is a no-op.

```java
import com.mongodb.client.model.Indexes;
import io.quarkus.runtime.Startup;
import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;
import jakarta.enterprise.context.ApplicationScoped;

@Startup
@ApplicationScoped
class MyCollectionIndexes {

@PostConstruct
    void ensureIndexes() {
        MyDocument.mongoCollection().createIndex(Indexes.ascending("scope"));
        // Add composite indexes, text indexes, etc. as needed
        // All calls are idempotent — safe to run on every startup
    }
}
```

The class can be package-private — it is not part of the public API.

**Why `mongoCollection()` from a static context works:** Panache MongoDB entity statics
delegate to the active CDI request scope at call time. In a `@PostConstruct` method,
CDI is fully initialized, so `mongoCollection()` resolves correctly.

### Why this is non-obvious
The Quarkus MongoDB docs don't describe a canonical startup initialization pattern for
indexes. The alternatives a developer typically reaches for:

- `@Observer(StartupEvent.class)` — valid but requires CDI event plumbing
- Out-of-band scripts — not reproducible in CI/new environments
- `@ApplicationScoped @PostConstruct` alone — bean may be lazy-initialized; indexes
  won't be created until the first injection point is activated, which may happen after
  the first query
- Flyway — not applicable to MongoDB

`@Startup` is the missing piece: it forces eager initialization regardless of whether
any other bean has injected this one. The combination with `@PostConstruct` keeps the
pattern explicit and testable.

**See also:** [GE-20260522-483b67](GE-20260522-483b67.md) — compound natural key string as @BsonId for upsert-by-identity

Dual-constructor aggregator: CDI constructor with ManagedExecutor + package-private test constructor for plain JUnit

## The Technique

Aggregators or services that use `ManagedExecutor` for CDI-context-safe parallel execution can expose a second package-private constructor for plain JUnit testing. The test constructor accepts contributors directly, sets executor to null, and switches to sequential execution:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class ActorStateAggregator {

private final List<ActorStateContributor> contributors;
    private final ManagedExecutor executor;

// CDI production constructor — parallel execution with CDI context propagation
    @Inject
    public ActorStateAggregator(
            @Any Instance<ActorStateContributor> contributors,
            ManagedExecutor executor) {
        this.contributors = contributors.stream().toList();
        this.executor = executor;
    }

// Test constructor — sequential execution, no executor needed
    ActorStateAggregator(List<ActorStateContributor> contributors) {
        this.contributors = contributors;
        this.executor = null;
    }

public ActorStateResponse forActor(String actorId) {
        if (executor != null) {
            // parallel via ManagedExecutor
        } else {
            // sequential for tests
            contributors.forEach(c -> runContributor(c, actorId, accumulator));
        }
    }
}
```

Test uses lambdas as contributor stubs — no CDI bootstrap required:
```java
var agg = new ActorStateAggregator(List.of(
    (id, acc) -> acc.trustScore(0.82),   // ledger stub
    (id, acc) -> { throw new RuntimeException("timeout"); }  // work stub
));
var resp = agg.forActor("agent-x");
assertThat(resp.sources()).doesNotContain("work");
```

## Why Non-obvious

Most developers reach for `@QuarkusTest + @InjectMock` to test CDI beans, which starts the full CDI container. For testing the aggregation logic itself (which contributors are called, how failures are isolated, what the response shape looks like), a plain JUnit test with the test constructor is faster and simpler. The key insight is that the dual-constructor pattern makes `executor=null` a valid sentinel that switches the implementation to a simpler sequential path — the behaviour under test (aggregation logic) is identical.

## Constraints

The test constructor must be package-private to prevent callers from bypassing CDI in production. The `executor=null` sentinel only works when sequential execution is semantically equivalent to parallel (no ordering dependencies between contributors). This pattern is distinct from GE-20260529-c1e783 (which uses `Consumer<T>` instead of `Event<T>` for CDI event firing) — here the bypass is `ManagedExecutor` rather than CDI events.

**See also:** [GE-20260529-c1e783](GE-20260529-c1e783.md) — dual-constructor CDI pattern: @Inject delegates to package-private Consumer<T> test constructor

**See also:** [GE-20260604-4bfd2c](GE-20260604-4bfd2c.md) — adding test constructor to field-injected CDI bean removes implicit no-arg (gotcha to avoid)

**See also:** [GE-20260609-75e00d](GE-20260609-75e00d.md) — package-private test constructor + @PostConstruct null guard technique

Remove JAX-RS coupling from @ApplicationScoped service beans using inner static domain exception classes

## Context

A `@Transactional @ApplicationScoped` service bean throws `ClientErrorException` or
`WebApplicationException` to signal not-found or conflict conditions to the calling JAX-RS
resource. This imports `jakarta.ws.rs.*` into the service layer — a layer violation:
services should not know about HTTP semantics.

The obvious alternatives all have problems:

- **`ClientErrorException` in service**: works at runtime but imports JAX-RS into service layer
- **Move validation to resource**: breaks `@Transactional` atomicity if validation and
  mutation must be atomic (TOCTOU: another request changes the entity between the check and
  the service call)
- **Checked exception hierarchy**: requires callers to catch or declare; awkward with
  `@Transactional` which only auto-rolls back on `RuntimeException`
- **`ExceptionMapper<MyException>`**: correct for cross-module use but adds infrastructure
  overhead for a module-local pattern

## Technique

Define lightweight inner static `RuntimeException` subclasses on the service itself.
They carry no HTTP knowledge. The JAX-RS resource catches them and maps to builder-based
responses — keeping HTTP concerns in the HTTP layer.

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class TrialActivationService {

public static class TrialNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
        private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;
        public TrialNotFoundException(UUID id) { super("Trial not found: " + id); }
    }

public static class TrialNotInPlanningStatusException extends RuntimeException {
        private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;
        public TrialNotInPlanningStatusException(TrialStatus status) {
            super("Trial status is " + status + ", expected PLANNING");
        }
    }

@Transactional
    Map<String, Object> markActive(UUID trialId) {
        ClinicalTrial trial = ClinicalTrial.findById(trialId);
        if (trial == null) throw new TrialNotFoundException(trialId);
        if (trial.status != TrialStatus.PLANNING) throw new TrialNotInPlanningStatusException(trial.status);
        // ... validation and mutation are atomic
    }
}

// In the JAX-RS resource:
public Response activate(@PathParam("id") UUID id) {
    try {
        trialActivationService.activate(id);
        return Response.noContent().build();
    } catch (TrialActivationService.TrialNotFoundException e) {
        return Response.status(Response.Status.NOT_FOUND).build();
    } catch (TrialActivationService.TrialNotInPlanningStatusException e) {
        return Response.status(Response.Status.CONFLICT).build();
    }
}
```

`RuntimeException` ensures `@Transactional` auto-rolls back on error. Inner static
placement means the exception is co-located with its thrower — one file to read for both
semantics and error conditions. The resource catch block is the single place where HTTP
status codes are decided.

## Why this is non-obvious

The instinct when a service needs to signal error conditions to HTTP callers is to reach
for `ClientErrorException` (it's in the JAX-RS API, it exists for exactly this) or to
move validation up to the resource layer. The inner static class pattern is not in any
JAX-RS guide because it's a layering discipline choice, not a JAX-RS feature.

The key insight is that the service's error taxonomy (`TrialNotFound`, `WrongStatus`)
is domain knowledge, not HTTP knowledge. The HTTP mapping (`NOT_FOUND`, `CONFLICT`) is the
resource's responsibility. Separating the two — domain exception on the service, HTTP
mapping on the resource — preserves both layer boundaries and `@Transactional` atomicity
without any infrastructure overhead.

@RequestScoped CDI bean silently fails in Quarkus @ConsumeEvent handlers — no error, no WorkItem

### Symptom
Integration test expects a WorkItem to be created after firing a case event. The assertion sees an empty store — `Expected size: 1 but was: 0`. No error, no exception, no log warning.

### Context
A `@ConsumeEvent(blocking = true)` handler calls `WorkItemService.create()`, which transitively injects `TenantScopedPrincipal` — a `@RequestScoped` bean. The handler runs on a Quarkus worker thread where no request context is active. CDI proxy throws `ContextNotActiveException` internally, but the event bus swallows it — the handler appears to complete normally.

The `@RequestScoped` bean wins CDI resolution over a `@DefaultBean @ApplicationScoped` alternative because non-default beans have higher priority. So the broken bean is always selected.

### What was tried (didn't work)
- Checking event bus wiring (correct — other handlers on the same bus work)
- Verifying WorkItemStore contents (empty — the create call never completes)
- Adding `@ActivateRequestContext` to the test method (doesn't help — handler runs on a different thread)

### Root cause
`@ConsumeEvent` handlers — even with `blocking = true` — do not activate a CDI request context. Any `@RequestScoped` bean in the injection chain throws `ContextNotActiveException`. The Vert.x event bus catches the exception and does not propagate it to the caller (fire-and-forget semantics).

### Fix
Exclude the `@RequestScoped` bean from the test CDI context and provide an `@ApplicationScoped` replacement:

```properties
# application.properties
quarkus.arc.exclude-types=io.casehub.work.runtime.service.TenantScopedPrincipal
```

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class NoOpCurrentPrincipal implements CurrentPrincipal {
    @Override public String actorId() { return "system"; }
    @Override public Set<String> groups() { return Set.of(); }
    @Override public String tenancyId() { return TenancyConstants.DEFAULT_TENANT_ID; }
    @Override public boolean isCrossTenantAdmin() { return false; }
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious
The failure is completely silent. No exception in logs, no test error beyond the assertion. The `@RequestScoped` bean wins CDI resolution automatically (higher priority than `@DefaultBean`), so you never suspect it's the problem. You debug the event bus, the handler logic, and the store — all correct. The request scope issue only surfaces when you trace the full injection chain.

@ApplicationScoped outer bean required for ContextNotActiveException catch to be reachable — @RequestScoped proxy throws before method entry

## Symptom

A CDI bean has a `try-catch(ContextNotActiveException)` inside a method body to
gracefully handle background-thread callers that have no request scope. The catch block
is never entered — background callers see the exception propagate despite the guard.

## Context

When implementing an HTTP-aware CDI bean that:
1. Reads from a `@RequestScoped` holder to provide per-request context (e.g. a tenant ID read from a request header), **and**
2. Must fall back gracefully (e.g. return a default value) when called from background threads (`@Scheduled`, `@ObservesAsync`, `StartupEvent` handlers) where no request scope is active.

## What was tried (didn't work)

Making the outer bean `@RequestScoped` with a try-catch inside its method body:

```java
// WRONG — @RequestScoped makes the catch unreachable for background callers
@RequestScoped
@Alternative
@Priority(1)
public class QhorusInboundCurrentPrincipal implements CurrentPrincipal {

@Inject
    InboundTenancyContext ctx;   // @RequestScoped holder

@Override
    public String tenancyId() {
        try {
            return ctx.tenancyId();          // this line is never reached from BG threads
        } catch (ContextNotActiveException e) {
            return TenancyConstants.DEFAULT_TENANT_ID;  // unreachable
        }
    }
}
```

Background callers that invoke `currentPrincipal.tenancyId()` get
`ContextNotActiveException` even though the method has a catch.

## Root cause

With `@RequestScoped` on the outer bean, the CDI **client proxy** (the object held by
callers) resolves to the request-scoped bean instance when methods are called. If no
request scope is active, **the proxy itself throws `ContextNotActiveException` before
the method body is entered**. The try-catch inside the method body is never reached —
it is outside the call stack entirely.

With `@ApplicationScoped`, the proxy always resolves (the application scope is always
active). The method body IS entered. Inside the body, accessing the `@RequestScoped`
inner holder (`ctx.tenancyId()`) throws `ContextNotActiveException` from within the
method body — where the catch CAN intercept it.

## Fix

Make the outer bean `@ApplicationScoped`. The per-request state lives in the
`@RequestScoped` inner holder; the outer bean is stateless and delegates:

```java
// CORRECT — @ApplicationScoped proxy always resolves; inner @RequestScoped access throws
// inside the method body where the catch fires
@ApplicationScoped
@Alternative
@Priority(1)
public class QhorusInboundCurrentPrincipal implements CurrentPrincipal {

@Inject
    InboundTenancyContext ctx;   // @RequestScoped — proxy resolves inside request, throws outside

@Override
    public String tenancyId() {
        try {
            return ctx.tenancyId();          // throws ContextNotActiveException for BG callers
        } catch (ContextNotActiveException e) {
            return TenancyConstants.DEFAULT_TENANT_ID;  // correctly reached
        }
    }
}

// Separate @RequestScoped holder for per-request state:
@RequestScoped
public class InboundTenancyContext {
    private String tenancyId = TenancyConstants.DEFAULT_TENANT_ID;
    public String tenancyId() { return tenancyId; }
    public void set(String t) { tenancyId = t != null && !t.isBlank() ? t : TenancyConstants.DEFAULT_TENANT_ID; }
}
```

For HTTP requests: `TenancyContextFilter @PreMatching` populates `InboundTenancyContext`.
For background callers: `ctx.tenancyId()` throws, the catch returns the default.

## Why this is non-obvious

The CDI spec and Quarkus documentation don't document the catch-reachability
implication of scope choice. A developer reasonably assumes that placing a try-catch
inside a method provides protection regardless of the bean's scope. The proxy's scope
check precedes method invocation — it is a consequence of how CDI client proxies work
(they must dispatch to a live scope instance; if the scope is inactive, they throw before
the target method is called at all). This is never mentioned in CDI or Quarkus docs for
the ARC implementation.

The failure is silent in production: the catch is not reached, the exception propagates,
and any background-thread caller that expected graceful degradation instead throws.
This can cause data isolation failures if the intent was to fall back to a safe default.

## Constraints

- Applies to any CDI proxy for a narrower scope (`@RequestScoped`, `@SessionScoped`,
  `@ConversationScoped`) injected into a wider scope (`@ApplicationScoped`,
  `@Singleton`) and accessed outside the active scope.
- The fix (split into outer `@ApplicationScoped` + inner `@RequestScoped` holder) only
  works when the outer bean is truly stateless and delegates to the inner holder.

**See also:** [GE-20260605-373190](GE-20260605-373190.md) — @ObservesAsync observer cannot inject @RequestScoped beans — ContextNotActiveException

Quartz job data map is the correct mechanism for threading execution context through job listeners without CDI request scope

### What a skilled developer would do instead

Inject the context via a CDI bean (`@RequestScoped` or `@ApplicationScoped`), set it before dispatching the job, and read it in the listener. This fails silently in Quartz listeners because they run on Quartz worker threads where CDI request scope is not active — `@RequestScoped` beans throw `ContextNotActiveException`, and `@ApplicationScoped` beans with thread-local state require manual setup that's easy to miss.

### Solution 1 — Quartz job data map (for Quartz job listeners)

**Approach:** Store context in the `JobDetail` data map at scheduling time; read it back in the listener on any thread.

**Pros:** Works across Quartz execution threads, retry cycles, and JDBC job stores. No CDI involvement.

**Cons/trade-offs:** Quartz-specific. Requires the context value to be known at scheduling time.

```java
// When scheduling the job (has access to tenancyId here)
JobDetail job = newJob(MyWorkerJob.class)
    .withIdentity(jobKey)
    .usingJobData("caseId", caseId.toString())
    .usingJobData("workerId", workerId)
    .usingJobData("tenancyId", tenancyId)   // ← thread through job data
    .build();

// In the JobListener (no CDI request scope here)
@Override
public void jobToBeExecuted(JobExecutionContext context) {
    String tenancyId = context.getMergedJobDataMap().getString("tenancyId");
    // use tenancyId directly — no CDI injection needed
    eventLogRepository.append(eventLog, tenancyId);
}

@Override
public void jobWasExecuted(JobExecutionContext context, JobExecutionException ex) {
    String tenancyId = context.getMergedJobDataMap().getString("tenancyId");
    // same data available at completion
}
```

Use `getMergedJobDataMap()` rather than `getJobDetail().getJobDataMap()` — the merged map combines job-level and trigger-level data, matching Quartz's resolution semantics.

### Solution 2 — Explicit method parameter (for @Scheduled or service methods)

**Approach:** Add `String tenancyId` as an explicit parameter to operations called from both request-context and no-request-context threads. HTTP callers pass the resolved `effectiveTenancyId`; scheduler callers pass `entity.tenancyId` (already stored on the entity at registration time).

**Pros:** Framework-agnostic. Works for `@Scheduled` methods, reactive pipelines, async observers, or any code that runs without a CDI request context. The entity already carries the tenancyId from when it was created in a proper request context.

**Cons/trade-offs:** Requires changing the method signature and all call sites. Callers must have tenancyId available — works when it's stored on a persistent entity (e.g. `Watchdog.tenancyId`), but not if the context is truly ephemeral.

```java
// Interface — explicit tenancyId param avoids CurrentPrincipal injection
public interface ChannelStore {
    void updateLastActivity(UUID channelId, String tenancyId);
}

// JPA implementation — uses the param, not CurrentPrincipal
@Override
@Transactional
public void updateLastActivity(UUID channelId, String tenancyId) {
    Channel.update("lastActivityAt = ?1 WHERE id = ?2 AND tenancyId = ?3",
            Instant.now(), channelId, tenancyId);
}

// HTTP request caller (has request context)
String effectiveTenancyId = dispatch.tenancyId() != null
        ? dispatch.tenancyId()
        : currentPrincipal.tenancyId();
channelStore.updateLastActivity(channel.id, effectiveTenancyId);

// Scheduler caller (no request context — reads from entity)
channelStore.updateLastActivity(channel.id, channel.tenancyId);
```

The key insight: the tenancyId was bound to the entity at registration time (when a request context *was* active). Reading it back from the entity is safe from any thread.

### Why this works (both solutions)

The common principle: capture context *at the time it's available* (request time / entity registration) and carry it explicitly through to where it's needed (listener / scheduler). CDI request scope is not the only mechanism — it's just the most convenient one when the request context is active.

### Context

Surfaced when adding `tenancyId` to a Quarkus/Quartz engine where `QuartzWorkerExecutionJobListener` needed to write tenant-scoped event log entries (Solution 1), and when `ChannelStore.updateLastActivity()` needed to be called from both `MessageService.dispatch()` (HTTP, request context active) and `WatchdogEvaluationService` (Quarkus `@Scheduled`, no request context) on the same method signature (Solution 2).

ExceptionMapper<IllegalArgumentException> does not catch compact constructor violations during Jackson deserialization — Jackson wraps them as ValueInstantiationException

### Symptom
REST endpoint continues to return 500 (or a generic Jackson error body) for invalid domain object input even after registering `@Provider ExceptionMapper<IllegalArgumentException>`. The mapper fires for `IllegalArgumentException` thrown from service methods but not for the same exception thrown from a record compact constructor during deserialization.

### Context
Using Java records with compact constructors for domain validation (e.g. a record that validates a score is in [0.0, 1.0]). When Jackson deserializes invalid JSON into the record, it invokes the canonical constructor which runs the compact constructor. If the compact constructor throws `IllegalArgumentException`, Jackson catches it and rethrows as `com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.exc.ValueInstantiationException extends JsonMappingException`.

### What was tried (didn't work)
Registered `@Provider @ApplicationScoped ExceptionMapper<IllegalArgumentException>` with `@Override toResponse()` returning HTTP 400 — worked for service-layer throws, missed the deserialization path entirely.

### Root cause
`ValueInstantiationException extends JsonMappingException`, not `IllegalArgumentException`. JAX-RS ExceptionMapper matching walks the thrown exception's type hierarchy — it does NOT inspect `getCause()`. The `ExceptionMapper<IllegalArgumentException>` is never invoked for `ValueInstantiationException` even though `exception.getCause() instanceof IllegalArgumentException` is true.

### Fix
Register two mappers in tandem:

1. `ExceptionMapper<IllegalArgumentException>` — handles direct service-layer throws.

2. `ExceptionMapper<JsonMappingException>` — handles Jackson-wrapped cases. Inspect `getCause()` and surface the domain message when the cause is `IllegalArgumentException`; return a generic 400 for other causes.

```java
@Provider
@ApplicationScoped
public class JsonMappingExceptionMapper implements ExceptionMapper<JsonMappingException> {
    @Override
    public Response toResponse(JsonMappingException exception) {
        final Throwable cause = exception.getCause();
        if (cause instanceof IllegalArgumentException iae) {
            final String msg = iae.getMessage() != null ? iae.getMessage() : "Bad request";
            return Response.status(BAD_REQUEST)
                    .type(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                    .entity(new ErrorResponse(msg))
                    .build();
        }
        return Response.status(BAD_REQUEST).build();
    }
}
```

