Jackson ObjectMapperCustomizer mixin adds @JsonTypeInfo to sealed interface without polluting pure-Java api/ module

### What this solves

You have a sealed interface in a pure-Java `api/` module (no framework dependencies).
Quarkus REST serializes it to JSON but produces no `"type"` discriminator field — REST
assertions on `response.body.field.type` return null. You cannot add `@JsonTypeInfo` to
the sealed interface because that would add Jackson as a dependency to the pure domain module.

### Technique

Register a Jackson mixin via `ObjectMapperCustomizer` in the `app/` module. The mixin
carries the Jackson annotations without touching the interface in `api/`:

```java
// In app/ — depends on Jackson, keeps api/ pure
@Singleton
public class DomainJacksonConfig implements ObjectMapperCustomizer {

@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = "type")
    @JsonSubTypes({
        @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = MyOutcome.Completed.class, name = "Completed"),
        @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = MyOutcome.Declined.class,  name = "Declined"),
        @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = MyOutcome.Failed.class,    name = "Failed")
    })
    interface MyOutcomeMixin {}

@Override
    public void customize(ObjectMapper mapper) {
        mapper.addMixIn(MyOutcome.class, MyOutcomeMixin.class);
    }
}
```

REST responses now include `{"type": "Completed", "result": {...}}` for sealed interface fields.

### Why this is non-obvious

Most developers either (a) add `@JsonTypeInfo` directly to the domain interface — violating
the hexagonal architecture rule that `api/` must have zero external dependencies — or (b)
add a DTO layer to avoid the issue entirely. `ObjectMapperCustomizer` is a Quarkus extension
point that most developers don't know exists; the Jackson mixin mechanism itself (`addMixIn`)
is a non-obvious Jackson API that applies annotations from one class to another at registration
time. The combination elegantly solves both problems with about 15 lines of code.

RESTEasy Reactive: return CompletionStage<Response> to chain async engine calls without @Blocking

### Context
A JAX-RS endpoint needs to call an async engine method that returns `CompletionStage<T>` (e.g. `CaseHub.startCase()` returning `CompletionStage<UUID>`), transform the result, and return an HTTP response — without blocking a Vert.x event thread.

### What developers normally do
Annotate the method with `@Blocking` and call `.toCompletableFuture().get()` — or use `Uni<Response>` and unwrap manually. Both either block a thread or require Mutiny familiarity.

### Technique
Return `CompletionStage<Response>` directly from the JAX-RS method. RESTEasy Reactive natively suspends the HTTP request on the event loop and resumes when the stage completes — no `@Blocking`, no `.get()`, no thread held:

```java
@POST
@Path("/cases/researcher")
public CompletionStage<Response> startResearcher() {
    if (engine.isUnsatisfied()) {
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(
            Response.status(Response.Status.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE)
                .entity(Map.of("error", "engine not available"))
                .build());
    }
    return engine.get()
        .startCase()
        .thenApply(caseId -> Response.accepted(new CaseStartedResponse(caseId)).build());
}
```

For the absent-engine 503 path, `CompletableFuture.completedFuture(...)` returns an already-resolved stage — RESTEasy Reactive handles it the same way, completing synchronously.

### Why this is non-obvious
`CompletionStage<Response>` as a JAX-RS return type is not prominent in Quarkus docs — examples focus on `Uni<Response>` (Mutiny) or blocking endpoints. The pattern works because RESTEasy Reactive checks the return type at augmentation time and generates a non-blocking handler for `CompletionStage`. No annotation, no framework import — just the JDK type.

